{"surah": 29, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "الٓمٓ ", "translation": "Alif, Lām, Meem.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "الٓمٓ", "translit": "ٓmٓlā", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "أَحَسِبَ ٱلنَّاسُ أَن يُتْرَكُوٓا۟ أَن يَقُولُوٓا۟ ءَامَنَّا وَهُمْ لَا يُفْتَنُونَ ", "translation": "Do the people think that they will be left to say, \"We believe\" and they will not be tried?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَحَسِبَ", "translit": "َbِsَḥَaʾ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُتْرَكُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُkَrْtُy", "root": "ترك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَقُولُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ءَامَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يُفْتَنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnَtْfُy", "root": "فتن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَلَقَدْ فَتَنَّا ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِهِمْۖ فَلَيَعْلَمَنَّ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ صَدَقُوا۟ وَلَيَعْلَمَنَّ ٱلْكَٰذِبِينَ ", "translation": "But We have certainly tried those before them, and Allāh will surely make evident those who are truthful, and He will surely make evident the liars.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَتَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَtَf", "root": "فتن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَلَيَعْلَمَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَmَlْʿعَyَlَf", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "صَدَقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَdَṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَيَعْلَمَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَmَlْʿعَyَlَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰذِبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbِhdَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كذب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "أَمْ حَسِبَ ٱلَّذِينَ يَعْمَلُونَ ٱلسَّيِّـَٔاتِ أَن يَسْبِقُونَاۚ سَآءَ مَا يَحْكُمُونَ ", "translation": "Or do those who do evil deeds think they can outrun [i.e., escape] Us? Evil is what they judge.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "حَسِبَ", "translit": "َbِsَḥ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّيِّـَٔاتِ", "translit": "ِtāَٔـِّyَّslٱ", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَسْبِقُونَا", "translit": "āَnwُqِbْsَy", "root": "سبق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "سَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَحْكُمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmُkْḥَy", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "مَن كَانَ يَرْجُوا۟ لِقَآءَ ٱللَّهِ فَإِنَّ أَجَلَ ٱللَّهِ لَءَاتٍۚ وَهُوَ ٱلسَّمِيعُ ٱلْعَلِيمُ ", "translation": "Whoever should hope for the meeting with Allāh - indeed, the term [decreed by] Allāh is coming. And He is the Hearing, the Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَرْجُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُjgْrَy", "root": "رجو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِقَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَqِl", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَجَلَ", "translit": "َlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَءَاتٍ", "translit": "ٍtāَءَl", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمِيعُ", "translit": "ُʿعyِmَّslٱ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْعَلِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِlَʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "وَمَن جَٰهَدَ فَإِنَّمَا يُجَٰهِدُ لِنَفْسِهِۦٓۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَغَنِىٌّ عَنِ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ ", "translation": "And whoever strives only strives for [the benefit of] himself. Indeed, Allāh is Free from need of the worlds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَٰهَدَ", "translit": "َdَhَٰjg", "root": "جهد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَإِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُجَٰهِدُ", "translit": "ُdِhَٰjgُy", "root": "جهد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِنَفْسِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِsْfَnِl", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَغَنِىٌّ", "translit": "ٌّàِnَغَl", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmَlَٰʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ لَنُكَفِّرَنَّ عَنْهُمْ سَيِّـَٔاتِهِمْ وَلَنَجْزِيَنَّهُمْ أَحْسَنَ ٱلَّذِى كَانُوا۟ يَعْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "And those who believe and do righteous deeds - We will surely remove from them their misdeeds and will surely reward them according to the best of what they used to do.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَنُكَفِّرَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَrِّfَkُnَl", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "سَيِّـَٔاتِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِtāَٔـِّyَs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَنَجْزِيَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَyِzْjgَnَlَw", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَحْسَنَ", "translit": "َnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَوَصَّيْنَا ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ بِوَٰلِدَيْهِ حُسْنًاۖ وَإِن جَٰهَدَاكَ لِتُشْرِكَ بِى مَا لَيْسَ لَكَ بِهِۦ عِلْمٌ فَلَا تُطِعْهُمَآۚ إِلَىَّ مَرْجِعُكُمْ فَأُنَبِّئُكُم بِمَا كُنتُمْ تَعْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "And We have enjoined upon man goodness to parents. But if they endeavor to make you associate with Me that of which you have no knowledge,[1] do not obey them. To Me is your return, and I will inform you about what you used to do.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَوَصَّيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَّṣَwَw", "root": "وصي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِوَٰلِدَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَdِlَٰwِb", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حُسْنًا", "translit": "āًnْsُḥ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "جَٰهَدَاكَ", "translit": "َkāَdَhَٰjg", "root": "جهد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لِتُشْرِكَ", "translit": "َkِrْhsُtِl", "root": "شرك"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِى", "translit": "àِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَl", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عِلْمٌ", "translit": "ٌmْlِʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تُطِعْهُمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmُhْʿعِṭُt", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَرْجِعُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُʿعِjgْrَm", "root": "رجع"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فَأُنَبِّئُكُم", "translit": "mُkُyʾِّbَnُaʾَf", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "تَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَt", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ لَنُدْخِلَنَّهُمْ فِى ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ ", "translation": "And those who believe and do righteous deeds - We will surely admit them among the righteous [into Paradise].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَنُدْخِلَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَlِhkْdُnَl", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ", "translit": "َnyِḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "وَمِنَ ٱلنَّاسِ مَن يَقُولُ ءَامَنَّا بِٱللَّهِ فَإِذَآ أُوذِىَ فِى ٱللَّهِ جَعَلَ فِتْنَةَ ٱلنَّاسِ كَعَذَابِ ٱللَّهِ وَلَئِن جَآءَ نَصْرٌ مِّن رَّبِّكَ لَيَقُولُنَّ إِنَّا كُنَّا مَعَكُمْۚ أَوَلَيْسَ ٱللَّهُ بِأَعْلَمَ بِمَا فِى صُدُورِ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ ", "translation": "And of the people are some who say, \"We believe in Allāh,\" but when one [of them] is harmed for [the cause of] Allāh, he considers the trial [i.e., harm] of the people as [if it were] the punishment of Allāh. But if victory comes from your Lord, they say, \"Indeed, We were with you.\" Is not Allāh most knowing of what is within the breasts of the worlds [i.e., all creatures]?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَامَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَإِذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُوذِىَ", "translit": "َàِhdwُaʾ", "root": "اذي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "جَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِتْنَةَ", "translit": "َẗَnْtِf", "root": "فتن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعَk", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَلَئِن", "translit": "nِyʾَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "نَصْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْṣَn", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "رَّبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "لَيَقُولُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُlwُqَyَl", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مَعَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "أَوَلَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَlَwَaʾ", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "بِأَعْلَمَ", "translit": "َmَlْʿعَaʾِb", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "صُدُورِ", "translit": "ِrwُdُṣ", "root": "صدر"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmَlَٰʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "وَلَيَعْلَمَنَّ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَلَيَعْلَمَنَّ ٱلْمُنَٰفِقِينَ ", "translation": "And Allāh will surely make evident those who believe, and He will surely make evident the hypocrites.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَيَعْلَمَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَmَlْʿعَyَlَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَيَعْلَمَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَmَlْʿعَyَlَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنَٰفِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِfَٰnُmْlٱ", "root": "نفق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "وَقَالَ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ لِلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ ٱتَّبِعُوا۟ سَبِيلَنَا وَلْنَحْمِلْ خَطَٰيَٰكُمْ وَمَا هُم بِحَٰمِلِينَ مِنْ خَطَٰيَٰهُم مِّن شَىْءٍۖ إِنَّهُمْ لَكَٰذِبُونَ ", "translation": "And those who disbelieve say to those who believe, \"Follow our way, and we will carry your sins.\"[1] But they will not carry anything of their sins. Indeed, they are liars.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱتَّبِعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعِbَّtٱ", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "سَبِيلَنَا", "translit": "āَnَlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلْنَحْمِلْ", "translit": "ْlِmْḥَnْlَw", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "خَطَٰيَٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَٰyَٰṭَhk", "root": "خطا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "هُم", "translit": "mُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِحَٰمِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِmَٰḥِb", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "خَطَٰيَٰهُم", "translit": "mُhَٰyَٰṭَhk", "root": "خطا"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَكَٰذِبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِhdَٰkَl", "root": "كذب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَلَيَحْمِلُنَّ أَثْقَالَهُمْ وَأَثْقَالًا مَّعَ أَثْقَالِهِمْۖ وَلَيُسْـَٔلُنَّ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ عَمَّا كَانُوا۟ يَفْتَرُونَ ", "translation": "But they will surely carry their [own] burdens and [other] burdens along with their burdens,[1] and they will surely be questioned on the Day of Resurrection about what they used to invent.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَيَحْمِلُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُlِmْḥَyَlَw", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَثْقَالَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlāَqْhtَaʾ", "root": "ثقل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَأَثْقَالًا", "translit": "āًlāَqْhtَaʾَw", "root": "ثقل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَّعَ", "translit": "َʿعَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَثْقَالِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlāَqْhtَaʾ", "root": "ثقل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلَيُسْـَٔلُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُlَٔـْsُyَlَw", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَفْتَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَtْfَy", "root": "فري"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "وَلَقَدْ أَرْسَلْنَا نُوحًا إِلَىٰ قَوْمِهِۦ فَلَبِثَ فِيهِمْ أَلْفَ سَنَةٍ إِلَّا خَمْسِينَ عَامًا فَأَخَذَهُمُ ٱلطُّوفَانُ وَهُمْ ظَٰلِمُونَ ", "translation": "And We certainly sent Noah to his people, and he remained among them a thousand years minus fifty years, and the flood seized them while they were wrongdoers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُوحًا", "translit": "āًḥwُn", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَوْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَلَبِثَ", "translit": "َhtِbَlَf", "root": "لبث"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَلْفَ", "translit": "َfْlَaʾ", "root": "الف"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سَنَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَnَs", "root": "سنو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "خَمْسِينَ", "translit": "َnyِsْmَhk", "root": "خمس"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَامًا", "translit": "āًmāَʿع", "root": "عوم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَأَخَذَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَhdَhkَaʾَf", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلطُّوفَانُ", "translit": "ُnāَfwُّṭlٱ", "root": "طوف"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ظَٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَٰẓ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "فَأَنجَيْنَٰهُ وَأَصْحَٰبَ ٱلسَّفِينَةِ وَجَعَلْنَٰهَآ ءَايَةً لِّلْعَٰلَمِينَ ", "translation": "But We saved him and the companions of the ship, and We made it[1] a sign for the worlds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَنجَيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَjgnَaʾَf", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَأَصْحَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰḥْṣَaʾَw", "root": "صحب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّفِينَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَnyِfَّslٱ", "root": "سفن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَٰهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhَٰnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَايَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِّلْعَٰلَمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmَlَٰʿعْlِّl", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "وَإِبْرَٰهِيمَ إِذْ قَالَ لِقَوْمِهِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَٱتَّقُوهُۖ ذَٰلِكُمْ خَيْرٌ لَّكُمْ إِن كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "And [We sent] Abraham, when he said to his people, \"Worship Allāh and fear Him. That is best for you, if you should know.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِقَوْمِهِ", "translit": "ِhِmْwَqِl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱعْبُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُbْʿعٱ", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "إِنَّمَا تَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ أَوْثَٰنًا وَتَخْلُقُونَ إِفْكًاۚ إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ تَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ لَا يَمْلِكُونَ لَكُمْ رِزْقًا فَٱبْتَغُوا۟ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرِّزْقَ وَٱعْبُدُوهُ وَٱشْكُرُوا۟ لَهُۥٓۖ إِلَيْهِ تُرْجَعُونَ ", "translation": "You only worship, besides Allāh, idols, and you produce a falsehood. Indeed, those you worship besides Allāh do not possess for you [the power of] provision. So seek from Allāh provision and worship Him and be grateful to Him. To Him you will be returned.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَعْبُدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdُbْʿعَt", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَوْثَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰhtْwَaʾ", "root": "وثن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَتَخْلُقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqُlْhkَtَw", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِفْكًا", "translit": "āًkْfِiʾ", "root": "افك"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَعْبُدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdُbْʿعَt", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَمْلِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِlْmَy", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "رِزْقًا", "translit": "āًqْzِr", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فَٱبْتَغُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُغَtْbٱَf", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلرِّزْقَ", "translit": "َqْzِّrlٱ", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَٱعْبُدُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُdُbْʿعٱَw", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَٱشْكُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُkْhsٱَw", "root": "شكر"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "تُرْجَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَjgْrُt", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "وَإِن تُكَذِّبُوا۟ فَقَدْ كَذَّبَ أُمَمٌ مِّن قَبْلِكُمْۖ وَمَا عَلَى ٱلرَّسُولِ إِلَّا ٱلْبَلَٰغُ ٱلْمُبِينُ ", "translation": "And if you [people] deny [the message] - already nations before you have denied. And there is not upon the Messenger except [the duty of] clear notification.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُكَذِّبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbِّhdَkُt", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَذَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أُمَمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَبْلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلرَّسُولِ", "translit": "ِlwُsَّrlٱ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْبَلَٰغُ", "translit": "ُغَٰlَbْlٱ", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْمُبِينُ", "translit": "ُnyِbُmْlٱ", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "أَوَلَمْ يَرَوْا۟ كَيْفَ يُبْدِئُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْخَلْقَ ثُمَّ يُعِيدُهُۥٓۚ إِنَّ ذَٰلِكَ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ يَسِيرٌ ", "translation": "Have they not considered how Allāh begins creation and then repeats it? Indeed that, for Allāh, is easy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَrَy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَk", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُبْدِئُ", "translit": "ُyʾِdْbُy", "root": "بدا"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْخَلْقَ", "translit": "َqْlَhkْlٱ", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُعِيدُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُdyِʿعُy", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَسِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِsَy", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "قُلْ سِيرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَٱنظُرُوا۟ كَيْفَ بَدَأَ ٱلْخَلْقَۚ ثُمَّ ٱللَّهُ يُنشِئُ ٱلنَّشْأَةَ ٱلْءَاخِرَةَۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ قَدِيرٌ ", "translation": "Say, [O Muḥammad], \"Travel through the land and observe how He began creation. Then Allāh will produce the final creation [i.e., development]. Indeed Allāh, over all things, is competent.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سِيرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُryِs", "root": "سير"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَٱنظُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُẓnٱَf", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَk", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَدَأَ", "translit": "َaʾَdَb", "root": "بدا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْخَلْقَ", "translit": "َqْlَhkْlٱ", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُنشِئُ", "translit": "ُyʾِhsnُy", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلنَّشْأَةَ", "translit": "َẗَaʾْhsَّnlٱ", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "قَدِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِdَq", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "يُعَذِّبُ مَن يَشَآءُ وَيَرْحَمُ مَن يَشَآءُۖ وَإِلَيْهِ تُقْلَبُونَ ", "translation": "He punishes whom He wills and has mercy upon whom He wills, and to Him you will be returned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يُعَذِّبُ", "translit": "ُbِّhdَʿعُy", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيَرْحَمُ", "translit": "ُmَḥْrَyَw", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَإِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تُقْلَبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbَlْqُt", "root": "قلب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "وَمَآ أَنتُم بِمُعْجِزِينَ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَلَا فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِۖ وَمَا لَكُم مِّن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ مِن وَلِىٍّ وَلَا نَصِيرٍ ", "translation": "And you will not cause failure [to Allāh] upon the earth or in the heaven. And you have not other than Allāh any protector or any helper.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنتُم", "translit": "mُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِمُعْجِزِينَ", "translit": "َnyِzِjgْʿعُmِb", "root": "عجز"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَلِىٍّ", "translit": "ٍّàِlَw", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "نَصِيرٍ", "translit": "ٍryِṣَn", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ بِـَٔايَٰتِ ٱللَّهِ وَلِقَآئِهِۦٓ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ يَئِسُوا۟ مِن رَّحْمَتِى وَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ لَهُمْ عَذَابٌ أَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "And the ones who disbelieve in the signs of Allāh and the meeting with Him - those have despaired of My mercy, and they will have a painful punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلِقَآئِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِyʾٓāَqِlَw", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَئِسُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُsِyʾَy", "root": "ياس"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَّحْمَتِى", "translit": "àِtَmْḥَّr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَذَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَaʾ", "root": "الم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "فَمَا كَانَ جَوَابَ قَوْمِهِۦٓ إِلَّآ أَن قَالُوا۟ ٱقْتُلُوهُ أَوْ حَرِّقُوهُ فَأَنجَىٰهُ ٱللَّهُ مِنَ ٱلنَّارِۚ إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَٰتٍ لِّقَوْمٍ يُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "And the answer of his [i.e., Abraham's] people was not but that they said, \"Kill him or burn him,\" but Allāh saved him from the fire. Indeed in that are signs for a people who believe.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَمَا", "translit": "āَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَوَابَ", "translit": "َbāَwَjg", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قَوْمِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱقْتُلُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُlُtْqٱ", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حَرِّقُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُqِّrَḥ", "root": "حرق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَأَنجَىٰهُ", "translit": "ُhٰàَjgnَaʾَf", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَءَايَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰyāَءَl", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لِّقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِّl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "وَقَالَ إِنَّمَا ٱتَّخَذْتُم مِّن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ أَوْثَٰنًا مَّوَدَّةَ بَيْنِكُمْ فِى ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ ٱلدُّنْيَاۖ ثُمَّ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ يَكْفُرُ بَعْضُكُم بِبَعْضٍ وَيَلْعَنُ بَعْضُكُم بَعْضًا وَمَأْوَىٰكُمُ ٱلنَّارُ وَمَا لَكُم مِّن نَّٰصِرِينَ ", "translation": "And [Abraham] said, \"You have only taken, other than Allāh, idols as [a bond of] affection among you in worldly life. Then on the Day of Resurrection you will deny one another and curse one another, and your refuge will be the Fire, and you will not have any helpers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذْتُم", "translit": "mُtْhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَوْثَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰhtْwَaʾ", "root": "وثن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَّوَدَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّdَwَّm", "root": "ودد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَيْنِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَكْفُرُ", "translit": "ُrُfْkَy", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بَعْضُكُم", "translit": "mُkُḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بِبَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَbِb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَيَلْعَنُ", "translit": "ُnَʿعْlَyَw", "root": "لعن"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "بَعْضُكُم", "translit": "mُkُḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "بَعْضًا", "translit": "āًḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَمَأْوَىٰكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkٰàَwْaʾَmَw", "root": "اوي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارُ", "translit": "ُrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "نَّٰصِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِṣَّٰn", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "فَـَٔامَنَ لَهُۥ لُوطٌۘ وَقَالَ إِنِّى مُهَاجِرٌ إِلَىٰ رَبِّىٓۖ إِنَّهُۥ هُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ ", "translation": "And Lot believed him. [Abraham] said, \"Indeed, I will emigrate to [the service of] my Lord. Indeed, He is the Exalted in Might, the Wise.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَـَٔامَنَ", "translit": "َnَmāَٔـَf", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لُوطٌ", "translit": "ٌṭwُl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُهَاجِرٌ", "translit": "ٌrِjgāَhُm", "root": "هجر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَبِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْعَزِيزُ", "translit": "ُzyِzَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحَكِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِkَḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "وَوَهَبْنَا لَهُۥٓ إِسْحَٰقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَجَعَلْنَا فِى ذُرِّيَّتِهِ ٱلنُّبُوَّةَ وَٱلْكِتَٰبَ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ أَجْرَهُۥ فِى ٱلدُّنْيَاۖ وَإِنَّهُۥ فِى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ لَمِنَ ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ ", "translation": "And We gave to him Isaac and Jacob and placed in his descendants prophethood and scripture. And We gave him his reward in this world, and indeed, he is in the Hereafter among the righteous.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَوَهَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَhَwَw", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِسْحَٰقَ", "translit": "َqَٰḥْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيَعْقُوبَ", "translit": "َbwُqْʿعَyَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ذُرِّيَّتِهِ", "translit": "ِhِtَّyِّrُhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلنُّبُوَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّwُbُّnlٱ", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱَw", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَجْرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrْjgَaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَإِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ", "translit": "َnyِḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "وَلُوطًا إِذْ قَالَ لِقَوْمِهِۦٓ إِنَّكُمْ لَتَأْتُونَ ٱلْفَٰحِشَةَ مَا سَبَقَكُم بِهَا مِنْ أَحَدٍ مِّنَ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ ", "translation": "And [mention] Lot, when he said to his people, \"Indeed, you commit such immorality as no one has preceded you with from among the worlds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلُوطًا", "translit": "āًṭwُlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِقَوْمِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِmْwَqِl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَتَأْتُونَ", "translit": "َnwُtْaʾَtَl", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْفَٰحِشَةَ", "translit": "َẗَhsِḥَٰfْlٱ", "root": "فحش"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سَبَقَكُم", "translit": "mُkَqَbَs", "root": "سبق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَحَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmَlَٰʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "أَئِنَّكُمْ لَتَأْتُونَ ٱلرِّجَالَ وَتَقْطَعُونَ ٱلسَّبِيلَ وَتَأْتُونَ فِى نَادِيكُمُ ٱلْمُنكَرَۖ فَمَا كَانَ جَوَابَ قَوْمِهِۦٓ إِلَّآ أَن قَالُوا۟ ٱئْتِنَا بِعَذَابِ ٱللَّهِ إِن كُنتَ مِنَ ٱلصَّٰدِقِينَ ", "translation": "Indeed, you approach men and obstruct the road[1] and commit in your meetings [every] evil.\" And the answer of his people was not but that they said, \"Bring us the punishment of Allāh, if you should be of the truthful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَئِنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnِyʾَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَتَأْتُونَ", "translit": "َnwُtْaʾَtَl", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلرِّجَالَ", "translit": "َlāَjgِّrlٱ", "root": "رجل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَتَقْطَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَṭْqَtَw", "root": "قطع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّبِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِbَّslٱ", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَتَأْتُونَ", "translit": "َnwُtْaʾَtَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَادِيكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkyِdāَn", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنكَرَ", "translit": "َrَknُmْlٱ", "root": "نكر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَمَا", "translit": "āَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "جَوَابَ", "translit": "َbāَwَjg", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "قَوْمِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱئْتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtْyʾٱ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بِعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعِb", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "كُنتَ", "translit": "َtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰدِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِdَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صدق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "قَالَ رَبِّ ٱنصُرْنِى عَلَى ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلْمُفْسِدِينَ ", "translation": "He said, \"My Lord, support me against the corrupting people.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱنصُرْنِى", "translit": "àِnْrُṣnٱ", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمُفْسِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِsْfُmْlٱ", "root": "فسد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَلَمَّا جَآءَتْ رُسُلُنَآ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ بِٱلْبُشْرَىٰ قَالُوٓا۟ إِنَّا مُهْلِكُوٓا۟ أَهْلِ هَٰذِهِ ٱلْقَرْيَةِۖ إِنَّ أَهْلَهَا كَانُوا۟ ظَٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And when Our messengers [i.e., angels] came to Abraham with the good tidings,[1] they said, \"Indeed, we will destroy the people of that [i.e., Lot's] city. Indeed, its people have been wrongdoers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَآءَتْ", "translit": "ْtَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رُسُلُنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnُlُsُr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْبُشْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhsُbْlٱِb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُهْلِكُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُkِlْhُm", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَهْلِ", "translit": "ِlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هَٰذِهِ", "translit": "ِhِhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْقَرْيَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَyْrَqْlٱ", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَهْلَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ظَٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَٰẓ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "قَالَ إِنَّ فِيهَا لُوطًاۚ قَالُوا۟ نَحْنُ أَعْلَمُ بِمَن فِيهَاۖ لَنُنَجِّيَنَّهُۥ وَأَهْلَهُۥٓ إِلَّا ٱمْرَأَتَهُۥ كَانَتْ مِنَ ٱلْغَٰبِرِينَ ", "translation": "[Abraham] said, \"Indeed, within it is Lot.\" They said, \"We are more knowing of who is within it. We will surely save him and his family, except his wife. She is to be of those who remain behind.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لُوطًا", "translit": "āًṭwُl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِمَن", "translit": "nَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَنُنَجِّيَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَyِّjgَnُnَl", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَأَهْلَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَlْhَaʾَw", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱمْرَأَتَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَtَaʾَrْmٱ", "root": "مرا"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَانَتْ", "translit": "ْtَnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْغَٰبِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِbَٰغْlٱ", "root": "غبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "وَلَمَّآ أَن جَآءَتْ رُسُلُنَا لُوطًا سِىٓءَ بِهِمْ وَضَاقَ بِهِمْ ذَرْعًا وَقَالُوا۟ لَا تَخَفْ وَلَا تَحْزَنْۖ إِنَّا مُنَجُّوكَ وَأَهْلَكَ إِلَّا ٱمْرَأَتَكَ كَانَتْ مِنَ ٱلْغَٰبِرِينَ ", "translation": "And when Our messengers [i.e., angels] came to Lot, he was distressed for them and felt for them great discomfort.[1] They said, \"Fear not, nor grieve. Indeed, we will save you and your family, except your wife; she is to be of those who remain behind.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَمَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَآءَتْ", "translit": "ْtَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رُسُلُنَا", "translit": "āَnُlُsُr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لُوطًا", "translit": "āًṭwُl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سِىٓءَ", "translit": "َءٓàِs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَضَاقَ", "translit": "َqāَḍَw", "root": "ضيق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ذَرْعًا", "translit": "āًʿعْrَhd", "root": "ذرع"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَقَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَخَفْ", "translit": "ْfَhkَt", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تَحْزَنْ", "translit": "ْnَzْḥَt", "root": "حزن"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مُنَجُّوكَ", "translit": "َkwُّjgَnُm", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَأَهْلَكَ", "translit": "َkَlْhَaʾَw", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱمْرَأَتَكَ", "translit": "َkَtَaʾَrْmٱ", "root": "مرا"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "كَانَتْ", "translit": "ْtَnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلْغَٰبِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِbَٰغْlٱ", "root": "غبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "إِنَّا مُنزِلُونَ عَلَىٰٓ أَهْلِ هَٰذِهِ ٱلْقَرْيَةِ رِجْزًا مِّنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ بِمَا كَانُوا۟ يَفْسُقُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, we will bring down on the people of this city punishment from the sky because they have been defiantly disobedient.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُنزِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِznُm", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَهْلِ", "translit": "ِlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هَٰذِهِ", "translit": "ِhِhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْقَرْيَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَyْrَqْlٱ", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رِجْزًا", "translit": "āًzْjgِr", "root": "رجز"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَفْسُقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqُsْfَy", "root": "فسق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "وَلَقَد تَّرَكْنَا مِنْهَآ ءَايَةًۢ بَيِّنَةً لِّقَوْمٍ يَعْقِلُونَ ", "translation": "And We have certainly left of it a sign as clear evidence for a people who use reason.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَد", "translit": "dَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَّرَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَrَّt", "root": "ترك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنْهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَايَةًۢ", "translit": "ًۢẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيِّنَةً", "translit": "ًẗَnِّyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِّقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِّl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْʿعَy", "root": "عقل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَإِلَىٰ مَدْيَنَ أَخَاهُمْ شُعَيْبًا فَقَالَ يَٰقَوْمِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَٱرْجُوا۟ ٱلْيَوْمَ ٱلْءَاخِرَ وَلَا تَعْثَوْا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ مُفْسِدِينَ ", "translation": "And to Madyan [We sent] their brother Shuʿayb, and he said, \"O my people, worship Allāh and expect the Last Day and do not commit abuse on the earth, spreading corruption.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَدْيَنَ", "translit": "َnَyْdَm", "root": "مدن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَخَاهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhāَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "شُعَيْبًا", "translit": "āًbْyَʿعُhs", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَٰقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqَٰy", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱعْبُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُbْʿعٱ", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱرْجُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُjgْrٱَw", "root": "رجو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyْlٱ", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَ", "translit": "َrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَعْثَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَhtْʿعَt", "root": "عثو"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مُفْسِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِsْfُm", "root": "فسد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "فَكَذَّبُوهُ فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلرَّجْفَةُ فَأَصْبَحُوا۟ فِى دَارِهِمْ جَٰثِمِينَ ", "translation": "But they denied him, so the earthquake seized them, and they became within their home [corpses] fallen prone.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَكَذَّبُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُbَّhdَkَf", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhْtَhdَhkَaʾَf", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلرَّجْفَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَfْjgَّrlٱ", "root": "رجف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأَصْبَحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥَbْṣَaʾَf", "root": "صبح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "دَارِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِrāَd", "root": "دور"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "جَٰثِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِhtَٰjg", "root": "جثم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "وَعَادًا وَثَمُودَا۟ وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْۖ وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ ", "translation": "And [We destroyed] ʿAad and Thamūd, and it has become clear to you from their [ruined] dwellings. And Satan had made pleasing to them their deeds and averted them from the path, and they were endowed with perception.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَعَادًا", "translit": "āًdāَʿعَw", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَثَمُودَا۟", "translit": "۟āَdwُmَhtَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَقَد", "translit": "dَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَّبَيَّنَ", "translit": "َnَّyَbَّt", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِnِkَٰsَّm", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَزَيَّنَ", "translit": "َnَّyَzَw", "root": "زين"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَصَدَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّdَṣَf", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلسَّبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَّslٱ", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَكَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِṣْbَtْsُm", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "وَقَٰرُونَ وَفِرْعَوْنَ وَهَٰمَٰنَۖ وَلَقَدْ جَآءَهُم مُّوسَىٰ بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ فَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَمَا كَانُوا۟ سَٰبِقِينَ ", "translation": "And [We destroyed] Qārūn and Pharaoh and Hāmān. And Moses had already come to them with clear evidences, and they were arrogant in the land, but they were not outrunners [of Our punishment].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَٰرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَٰqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَفِرْعَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَʿعْrِfَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَهَٰمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmَٰhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جَآءَهُم", "translit": "mُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُّوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰnِّyَbْlٱِb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَbْkَtْsٱَf", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سَٰبِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِbَٰs", "root": "سبق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "فَكُلًّا أَخَذْنَا بِذَنۢبِهِۦۖ فَمِنْهُم مَّنْ أَرْسَلْنَا عَلَيْهِ حَاصِبًا وَمِنْهُم مَّنْ أَخَذَتْهُ ٱلصَّيْحَةُ وَمِنْهُم مَّنْ خَسَفْنَا بِهِ ٱلْأَرْضَ وَمِنْهُم مَّنْ أَغْرَقْنَاۚ وَمَا كَانَ ٱللَّهُ لِيَظْلِمَهُمْ وَلَٰكِن كَانُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَهُمْ يَظْلِمُونَ ", "translation": "So each We seized for his sin; and among them were those upon whom We sent a storm of stones, and among them were those who were seized by the blast [from the sky], and among them were those whom We caused the earth to swallow, and among them were those whom We drowned. And Allāh would not have wronged them, but it was they who were wronging themselves.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَكُلًّا", "translit": "āًّlُkَf", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَخَذْنَا", "translit": "āَnْhdَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِذَنۢبِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِbۢnَhdِb", "root": "ذنب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَمِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "حَاصِبًا", "translit": "āًbِṣāَḥ", "root": "حصب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَمِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَخَذَتْهُ", "translit": "ُhْtَhdَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلصَّيْحَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَḥْyَّṣlٱ", "root": "صيح"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَمِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "خَسَفْنَا", "translit": "āَnْfَsَhk", "root": "خسف"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بِهِ", "translit": "ِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَمِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَغْرَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَrْغَaʾ", "root": "غرق"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "لِيَظْلِمَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَmِlْẓَyِl", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "كَانُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "أَنفُسَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "يَظْلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlْẓَy", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "مَثَلُ ٱلَّذِينَ ٱتَّخَذُوا۟ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ أَوْلِيَآءَ كَمَثَلِ ٱلْعَنكَبُوتِ ٱتَّخَذَتْ بَيْتًاۖ وَإِنَّ أَوْهَنَ ٱلْبُيُوتِ لَبَيْتُ ٱلْعَنكَبُوتِۖ لَوْ كَانُوا۟ يَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "The example of those who take allies other than Allāh is like that of the spider who takes [i.e., constructs] a home. And indeed, the weakest of homes is the home of the spider, if they only knew.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَثَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhtَm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَوْلِيَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَyِlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَمَثَلِ", "translit": "ِlَhtَmَk", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْعَنكَبُوتِ", "translit": "ِtwُbَknَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عنكب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذَتْ", "translit": "ْtَhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَيْتًا", "translit": "āًtْyَb", "root": "بيت"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَوْهَنَ", "translit": "َnَhْwَaʾ", "root": "وهن"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْبُيُوتِ", "translit": "ِtwُyُbْlٱ", "root": "بيت"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَبَيْتُ", "translit": "ُtْyَbَl", "root": "بيت"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْعَنكَبُوتِ", "translit": "ِtwُbَknَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عنكب"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ مَا يَدْعُونَ مِن دُونِهِۦ مِن شَىْءٍۚ وَهُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ ", "translation": "Indeed, Allāh knows whatever thing they call upon[1] other than Him. And He is the Exalted in Might, the Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَدْعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعْdَy", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْعَزِيزُ", "translit": "ُzyِzَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحَكِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِkَḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "وَتِلْكَ ٱلْأَمْثَٰلُ نَضْرِبُهَا لِلنَّاسِۖ وَمَا يَعْقِلُهَآ إِلَّا ٱلْعَٰلِمُونَ ", "translation": "And these examples We present to the people, but none will understand them except those of knowledge.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَتِلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlِtَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْثَٰلُ", "translit": "ُlَٰhtْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَضْرِبُهَا", "translit": "āَhُbِrْḍَn", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَعْقِلُهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhُlِqْʿعَy", "root": "عقل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْعَٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَٰʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "خَلَقَ ٱللَّهُ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ بِٱلْحَقِّۚ إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَةً لِّلْمُؤْمِنِينَ ", "translation": "Allāh created the heavens and the earth in truth. Indeed in that is a sign for the believers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَءَايَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyāَءَl", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِّلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlِّl", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "ٱتْلُ مَآ أُوحِىَ إِلَيْكَ مِنَ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ وَأَقِمِ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَۖ إِنَّ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ تَنْهَىٰ عَنِ ٱلْفَحْشَآءِ وَٱلْمُنكَرِۗ وَلَذِكْرُ ٱللَّهِ أَكْبَرُۗ وَٱللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ مَا تَصْنَعُونَ ", "translation": "Recite, [O Muḥammad], what has been revealed to you of the Book and establish prayer. Indeed, prayer prohibits immorality and wrongdoing, and the remembrance of Allāh is greater. And Allāh knows that which you do.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱتْلُ", "translit": "ُlْtٱ", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أُوحِىَ", "translit": "َàِḥwُaʾ", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَقِمِ", "translit": "ِmِqَaʾَw", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَنْهَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhْnَt", "root": "نهي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْفَحْشَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَhsْḥَfْlٱ", "root": "فحش"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُنكَرِ", "translit": "ِrَknُmْlٱَw", "root": "نكر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَلَذِكْرُ", "translit": "ُrْkِhdَlَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَكْبَرُ", "translit": "ُrَbْkَaʾ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "تَصْنَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَnْṣَt", "root": "صنع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "وَلَا تُجَٰدِلُوٓا۟ أَهْلَ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ إِلَّا بِٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُ إِلَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا۟ مِنْهُمْۖ وَقُولُوٓا۟ ءَامَنَّا بِٱلَّذِىٓ أُنزِلَ إِلَيْنَا وَأُنزِلَ إِلَيْكُمْ وَإِلَٰهُنَا وَإِلَٰهُكُمْ وَٰحِدٌ وَنَحْنُ لَهُۥ مُسْلِمُونَ ", "translation": "And do not argue with the People of the Scripture except in a way that is best, except for those who commit injustice among them, and say, \"We believe in that which has been revealed to us and revealed to you. And our God and your God is one; and we are Muslims [in submission] to Him.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُجَٰدِلُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlِdَٰjgُt", "root": "جدل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَهْلَ", "translit": "َlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَحْسَنُ", "translit": "ُnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ظَلَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَlَẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَقُولُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlwُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ءَامَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بِٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَأُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾَw", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَإِلَٰهُنَا", "translit": "āَnُhَٰlِiʾَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَإِلَٰهُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُhَٰlِiʾَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَٰحِدٌ", "translit": "ٌdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَنَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَnَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "مُسْلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlْsُm", "root": "سلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "وَكَذَٰلِكَ أَنزَلْنَآ إِلَيْكَ ٱلْكِتَٰبَۚ فَٱلَّذِينَ ءَاتَيْنَٰهُمُ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ يُؤْمِنُونَ بِهِۦۖ وَمِنْ هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ مَن يُؤْمِنُ بِهِۦۚ وَمَا يَجْحَدُ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآ إِلَّا ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ ", "translation": "And thus We have sent down to you the Book [i.e., the Qur’ān]. And those to whom We [previously] gave the Scripture believe in it. And among these [people of Makkah] are those who believe in it. And none reject Our verses except the disbelievers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَٰهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَٰnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمِنْ", "translit": "ْnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُ", "translit": "ُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَجْحَدُ", "translit": "ُdَḥْjgَy", "root": "جحد"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "وَمَا كُنتَ تَتْلُوا۟ مِن قَبْلِهِۦ مِن كِتَٰبٍ وَلَا تَخُطُّهُۥ بِيَمِينِكَۖ إِذًا لَّٱرْتَابَ ٱلْمُبْطِلُونَ ", "translation": "And you did not recite before it any scripture, nor did you inscribe one with your right hand. Then [i.e., otherwise] the falsifiers would have had [cause for] doubt.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كُنتَ", "translit": "َtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَتْلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlْtَt", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كِتَٰبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَخُطُّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُّṭُhkَt", "root": "خطط"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِيَمِينِكَ", "translit": "َkِnyِmَyِb", "root": "يمن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَّٱرْتَابَ", "translit": "َbāَtْrٱَّl", "root": "ريب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْمُبْطِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِṭْbُmْlٱ", "root": "بطل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "بَلْ هُوَ ءَايَٰتٌۢ بَيِّنَٰتٌ فِى صُدُورِ ٱلَّذِينَ أُوتُوا۟ ٱلْعِلْمَۚ وَمَا يَجْحَدُ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآ إِلَّا ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ ", "translation": "Rather, it [i.e., the Qur’ān] is distinct verses [preserved] within the breasts of those who have been given knowledge. And none reject Our verses except the wrongdoers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَيِّنَٰتٌ", "translit": "ٌtَٰnِّyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "صُدُورِ", "translit": "ِrwُdُṣ", "root": "صدر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُوتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtwُaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمَ", "translit": "َmْlِʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَجْحَدُ", "translit": "ُdَḥْjgَy", "root": "جحد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "وَقَالُوا۟ لَوْلَآ أُنزِلَ عَلَيْهِ ءَايَٰتٌ مِّن رَّبِّهِۦۖ قُلْ إِنَّمَا ٱلْءَايَٰتُ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ وَإِنَّمَآ أَنَا۠ نَذِيرٌ مُّبِينٌ ", "translation": "But they say, \"Why are not signs sent down to him from his Lord?\" Say, \"The signs are only with Allāh, and I am only a clear warner.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَوْلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتٌ", "translit": "ٌtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْءَايَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰyāَءْlٱ", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَإِنَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "نَذِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِhdَn", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مُّبِينٌ", "translit": "ٌnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "أَوَلَمْ يَكْفِهِمْ أَنَّآ أَنزَلْنَا عَلَيْكَ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ يُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْهِمْۚ إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَرَحْمَةً وَذِكْرَىٰ لِقَوْمٍ يُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "And is it not sufficient for them that We revealed to you the Book [i.e., the Qur’ān] which is recited to them? Indeed in that is a mercy and reminder for a people who believe.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَكْفِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِfْkَy", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُتْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْtُy", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَرَحْمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmْḥَrَl", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَذِكْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْkِhdَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لِقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "قُلْ كَفَىٰ بِٱللَّهِ بَيْنِى وَبَيْنَكُمْ شَهِيدًاۖ يَعْلَمُ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۗ وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ بِٱلْبَٰطِلِ وَكَفَرُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْخَٰسِرُونَ ", "translation": "Say, \"Sufficient is Allāh between me and you as Witness. He knows what is in the heavens and earth. And they who have believed in falsehood and disbelieved in Allāh - it is those who are the losers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَk", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَيْنِى", "translit": "àِnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَبَيْنَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَnْyَbَw", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "شَهِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِٱلْبَٰطِلِ", "translit": "ِlِṭَٰbْlٱِb", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَكَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَkَw", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْخَٰسِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِsَٰhkْlٱ", "root": "خسر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 53, "text": "وَيَسْتَعْجِلُونَكَ بِٱلْعَذَابِۚ وَلَوْلَآ أَجَلٌ مُّسَمًّى لَّجَآءَهُمُ ٱلْعَذَابُ وَلَيَأْتِيَنَّهُم بَغْتَةً وَهُمْ لَا يَشْعُرُونَ ", "translation": "And they urge you to hasten the punishment. And if not for [the decree of] a specified term, punishment would have reached them. But it will surely come to them suddenly while they perceive not.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَسْتَعْجِلُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُlِjgْʿعَtْsَyَw", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلْعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعْlٱِb", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلَوْلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَجَلٌ", "translit": "ٌlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُّسَمًّى", "translit": "àًّmَsُّm", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَّجَآءَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَءٓāَjgَّl", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْعَذَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَhdَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَيَأْتِيَنَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّnَyِtْaʾَyَlَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَغْتَةً", "translit": "ًẗَtْغَb", "root": "بغت"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَشْعُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُʿعْhsَy", "root": "شعر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 54, "text": "يَسْتَعْجِلُونَكَ بِٱلْعَذَابِ وَإِنَّ جَهَنَّمَ لَمُحِيطَةٌۢ بِٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ ", "translation": "They urge you to hasten the punishment. And indeed, Hell will be encompassing of the disbelievers", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَسْتَعْجِلُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُlِjgْʿعَtْsَy", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلْعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعْlٱِb", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَمُحِيطَةٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢẗَṭyِḥُmَl", "root": "حوط"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlٱِb", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 55, "text": "يَوْمَ يَغْشَىٰهُمُ ٱلْعَذَابُ مِن فَوْقِهِمْ وَمِن تَحْتِ أَرْجُلِهِمْ وَيَقُولُ ذُوقُوا۟ مَا كُنتُمْ تَعْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "On the Day the punishment will cover them from above them and from below their feet and it is said, \"Taste [the result of] what you used to do.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَغْشَىٰهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhٰàَhsْغَy", "root": "غشو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْعَذَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَhdَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَوْقِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِqْwَf", "root": "فوق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمِن", "translit": "nِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَحْتِ", "translit": "ِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَرْجُلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlُjgْrَaʾ", "root": "رجل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ذُوقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqwُhd", "root": "ذوق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَt", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 56, "text": "يَٰعِبَادِىَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ إِنَّ أَرْضِى وَٰسِعَةٌ فَإِيَّٰىَ فَٱعْبُدُونِ ", "translation": "O My servants who have believed, indeed My earth is spacious, so worship only Me.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰعِبَادِىَ", "translit": "َàِdāَbِʿعَٰy", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَرْضِى", "translit": "àِḍْrَaʾ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٰسِعَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَʿعِsَٰw", "root": "وسع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَإِيَّٰىَ", "translit": "َàَّٰyِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَٱعْبُدُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُdُbْʿعٱَf", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 57, "text": "كُلُّ نَفْسٍ ذَآئِقَةُ ٱلْمَوْتِۖ ثُمَّ إِلَيْنَا تُرْجَعُونَ ", "translation": "Every soul will taste death. Then to Us will you be returned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَفْسٍ", "translit": "ٍsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذَآئِقَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَqِyʾٓāَhd", "root": "ذوق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتِ", "translit": "ِtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُرْجَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَjgْrُt", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 58, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ لَنُبَوِّئَنَّهُم مِّنَ ٱلْجَنَّةِ غُرَفًا تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ خَٰلِدِينَ فِيهَاۚ نِعْمَ أَجْرُ ٱلْعَٰمِلِينَ ", "translation": "And those who have believed and done righteous deeds - We will surely assign to them of Paradise [elevated] chambers beneath which rivers flow, wherein they abide eternally. Excellent is the reward of the [righteous] workers", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَنُبَوِّئَنَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّnَyʾِّwَbُnَl", "root": "بوا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْجَنَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّnَjgْlٱ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "غُرَفًا", "translit": "āًfَrُغ", "root": "غرف"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "خَٰلِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "نِعْمَ", "translit": "َmْʿعِn", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَجْرُ", "translit": "ُrْjgَaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْعَٰمِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِmَٰʿعْlٱ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 59, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ صَبَرُوا۟ وَعَلَىٰ رَبِّهِمْ يَتَوَكَّلُونَ ", "translation": "Who have been patient and upon their Lord rely.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَبَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَbَṣ", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿعَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَتَوَكَّلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَّkَwَtَy", "root": "وكل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 60, "text": "وَكَأَيِّن مِّن دَآبَّةٍ لَّا تَحْمِلُ رِزْقَهَا ٱللَّهُ يَرْزُقُهَا وَإِيَّاكُمْۚ وَهُوَ ٱلسَّمِيعُ ٱلْعَلِيمُ ", "translation": "And how many a creature carries not its [own] provision. Allāh provides for it and for you. And He is the Hearing, the Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَأَيِّن", "translit": "nِّyَaʾَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "دَآبَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّbٓāَd", "root": "دبب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَحْمِلُ", "translit": "ُlِmْḥَt", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رِزْقَهَا", "translit": "āَhَqْzِr", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَرْزُقُهَا", "translit": "āَhُqُzْrَy", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَإِيَّاكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkāَّyِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمِيعُ", "translit": "ُʿعyِmَّslٱ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْعَلِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِlَʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 61, "text": "وَلَئِن سَأَلْتَهُم مَّنْ خَلَقَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ وَسَخَّرَ ٱلشَّمْسَ وَٱلْقَمَرَ لَيَقُولُنَّ ٱللَّهُۖ فَأَنَّىٰ يُؤْفَكُونَ ", "translation": "If you asked them, \"Who created the heavens and earth and subjected the sun and the moon?\" they would surely say, \"Allāh.\" Then how are they deluded?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَئِن", "translit": "nِyʾَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَأَلْتَهُم", "translit": "mُhَtْlَaʾَs", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَسَخَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّhkَsَw", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلشَّمْسَ", "translit": "َsْmَّhslٱ", "root": "شمس"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱلْقَمَرَ", "translit": "َrَmَqْlٱَw", "root": "قمر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَيَقُولُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُlwُqَyَl", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَأَنَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّnَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُؤْفَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَfْwʾُy", "root": "افك"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 62, "text": "ٱللَّهُ يَبْسُطُ ٱلرِّزْقَ لِمَن يَشَآءُ مِنْ عِبَادِهِۦ وَيَقْدِرُ لَهُۥٓۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ بِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ عَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "Allāh extends provision for whom He wills of His servants and restricts for him. Indeed Allāh is, of all things, Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَبْسُطُ", "translit": "ُṭُsْbَy", "root": "بسط"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلرِّزْقَ", "translit": "َqْzِّrlٱ", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِمَن", "translit": "nَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عِبَادِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَيَقْدِرُ", "translit": "ُrِdْqَyَw", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِb", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 63, "text": "وَلَئِن سَأَلْتَهُم مَّن نَّزَّلَ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءً فَأَحْيَا بِهِ ٱلْأَرْضَ مِنۢ بَعْدِ مَوْتِهَا لَيَقُولُنَّ ٱللَّهُۚ قُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِۚ بَلْ أَكْثَرُهُمْ لَا يَعْقِلُونَ ", "translation": "And if you asked them, \"Who sends down rain from the sky and gives life thereby to the earth after its lifelessness?\" they would surely say, \"Allāh.\" Say, \"Praise to Allāh\"; but most of them do not reason.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَئِن", "translit": "nِyʾَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَأَلْتَهُم", "translit": "mُhَtْlَaʾَs", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّن", "translit": "nَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَّزَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّzَّn", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَm", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَأَحْيَا", "translit": "āَyْḥَaʾَf", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِهِ", "translit": "ِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَوْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْwَm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَيَقُولُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُlwُqَyَl", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "قُلِ", "translit": "ِlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْحَمْدُ", "translit": "ُdْmَḥْlٱ", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَكْثَرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrَhtْkَaʾ", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "يَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْʿعَy", "root": "عقل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 64, "text": "وَمَا هَٰذِهِ ٱلْحَيَوٰةُ ٱلدُّنْيَآ إِلَّا لَهْوٌ وَلَعِبٌۚ وَإِنَّ ٱلدَّارَ ٱلْءَاخِرَةَ لَهِىَ ٱلْحَيَوَانُۚ لَوْ كَانُوا۟ يَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "And this worldly life is not but diversion and amusement. And indeed, the home of the Hereafter - that is the [eternal] life, if only they knew.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَٰذِهِ", "translit": "ِhِhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةُ", "translit": "ُẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَآ", "translit": "ٓāَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهْوٌ", "translit": "ٌwْhَl", "root": "لهو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَعِبٌ", "translit": "ٌbِʿعَlَw", "root": "لعب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلدَّارَ", "translit": "َrāَّdlٱ", "root": "دور"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهِىَ", "translit": "َàِhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوَانُ", "translit": "ُnāَwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 65, "text": "فَإِذَا رَكِبُوا۟ فِى ٱلْفُلْكِ دَعَوُا۟ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِصِينَ لَهُ ٱلدِّينَ فَلَمَّا نَجَّىٰهُمْ إِلَى ٱلْبَرِّ إِذَا هُمْ يُشْرِكُونَ ", "translation": "And when they board a ship, they supplicate Allāh, sincere to Him in religion [i.e., faith and hope]. But when He delivers them to the land, at once they associate others with Him", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَكِبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbِkَr", "root": "ركب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْفُلْكِ", "translit": "ِkْlُfْlٱ", "root": "فلك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "دَعَوُا۟", "translit": "۟āُwَʿعَd", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُخْلِصِينَ", "translit": "َnyِṣِlْhkُm", "root": "خلص"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَهُ", "translit": "ُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلدِّينَ", "translit": "َnyِّdlٱ", "root": "دين"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "نَجَّىٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhٰàَّjgَn", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْبَرِّ", "translit": "ِّrَbْlٱ", "root": "برر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يُشْرِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِrْhsُy", "root": "شرك"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 66, "text": "لِيَكْفُرُوا۟ بِمَآ ءَاتَيْنَٰهُمْ وَلِيَتَمَتَّعُوا۟ۖ فَسَوْفَ يَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "So that they will deny what We have granted them, and they will enjoy themselves. But they are going to know.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِيَكْفُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُfْkَyِl", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلِيَتَمَتَّعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعَّtَmَtَyِlَw", "root": "متع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَسَوْفَ", "translit": "َfْwَsَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 67, "text": "أَوَلَمْ يَرَوْا۟ أَنَّا جَعَلْنَا حَرَمًا ءَامِنًا وَيُتَخَطَّفُ ٱلنَّاسُ مِنْ حَوْلِهِمْۚ أَفَبِٱلْبَٰطِلِ يُؤْمِنُونَ وَبِنِعْمَةِ ٱللَّهِ يَكْفُرُونَ ", "translation": "Have they not seen that We made [Makkah] a safe sanctuary, while people are being taken away[1] all around them? Then in falsehood do they believe, and in the favor of Allāh they disbelieve?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَrَy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حَرَمًا", "translit": "āًmَrَḥ", "root": "حرم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَامِنًا", "translit": "āًnِmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيُتَخَطَّفُ", "translit": "ُfَّṭَhkَtُyَw", "root": "خطف"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حَوْلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlْwَḥ", "root": "حول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَفَبِٱلْبَٰطِلِ", "translit": "ِlِṭَٰbْlٱِbَfَaʾ", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَبِنِعْمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmْʿعِnِbَw", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَكْفُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُfْkَy", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 68, "text": "وَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّنِ ٱفْتَرَىٰ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ كَذِبًا أَوْ كَذَّبَ بِٱلْحَقِّ لَمَّا جَآءَهُۥٓۚ أَلَيْسَ فِى جَهَنَّمَ مَثْوًى لِّلْكَٰفِرِينَ ", "translation": "And who is more unjust than one who invents a lie about Allāh or denies the truth when it has come to him? Is there not in Hell a [sufficient] residence for the disbelievers?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَظْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْẓَaʾ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِمَّنِ", "translit": "ِnَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱفْتَرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrَtْfٱ", "root": "فري"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَذِبًا", "translit": "āًbِhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَذَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "جَآءَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَلَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَlَaʾ", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مَثْوًى", "translit": "àًwْhtَm", "root": "ثوي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لِّلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlِّl", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 69, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ جَٰهَدُوا۟ فِينَا لَنَهْدِيَنَّهُمْ سُبُلَنَاۚ وَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَمَعَ ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ ", "translation": "And those who strive for Us - We will surely guide them to Our ways.[1] And indeed, Allāh is with the doers of good.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَٰهَدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdَhَٰjg", "root": "جهد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِينَا", "translit": "āَnyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَنَهْدِيَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَyِdْhَnَl", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سُبُلَنَا", "translit": "āَnَlُbُs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَمَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِsْḥُmْlٱ", "root": "حسن"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"7": [{"arabic": "دونه وادعى الإجماع فيه واستدل بأدلة تشعر بنقيض غرضه كقوله تعالى {الذين آمنوا وعملوا الصالحات} إذ هذا يدل على أن العمل وراء الإيمان لا من نفس الإيمان وإلا فيكون العمل في حكم المعاد والعجب أنه ادعى الإجماع في هذا وهو مع ذلك ينقل قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يكفر أحد إلا بعد جحوده لما أقر به (1) وينكر على المعتزلة قولهم بالتخليد في النار بسبب الكبائر والقائل بهذا قائل بنفس مذهب المعتزلة إذ يقال له من صدق بقلبه وشهد بلسانه ومات في الحال فهل هو في الجنة فلا بد أن يقول نعم وفيه حكم بوجود الإيمان دون العمل فنزيد ونقول لو بقي حيا حتى دخل عليه وقت", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "9": [{"arabic": "دونه وادعى الإجماع فيه واستدل بأدلة تشعر بنقيض غرضه كقوله تعالى {الذين آمنوا وعملوا الصالحات} إذ هذا يدل على أن العمل وراء الإيمان لا من نفس الإيمان وإلا فيكون العمل في حكم المعاد والعجب أنه ادعى الإجماع في هذا وهو مع ذلك ينقل قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يكفر أحد إلا بعد جحوده لما أقر به (1) وينكر على المعتزلة قولهم بالتخليد في النار بسبب الكبائر والقائل بهذا قائل بنفس مذهب المعتزلة إذ يقال له من صدق بقلبه وشهد بلسانه ومات في الحال فهل هو في الجنة فلا بد أن يقول نعم وفيه حكم بوجود الإيمان دون العمل فنزيد ونقول لو بقي حيا حتى دخل عليه وقت", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "17": [{"arabic": "مع كونه عبدا مثله ذل قبيح قال الله تعالى {إن الذين تعبدون من دون الله لا يملكون لكم رزقا فابتغوا عند الله الرزق واعبدوه واشكروا له} وقال تعالى {إن الذين تدعون من دون الله عباد أمثالكم} فالشكر باللسان من جملة الشكر وقد روي أن وفدا قدموا على عمر بن عبد العزيز رحمه الله فقام شاب ليتكلم فقال عمر الكبر الكبر فقال يا أمير المؤمنين لو كان الأمر بالسن لكان في المسلمين من هو أسكن منك فقال تكلم فقال لسنا وفد الرغبة ولا وفد الرهبة أما الرغبة فقد أوصلها إلينا فضلك وأما الرهبة فقد آمننا منها عدلك وإنما نحن وقد الشكر جئناك نشكرك باللسان وننصرف فهذه هي أصول معاني الشكر المحيطة بمجموع حقيقته", "english": "...not the vision of the blessing. And al-Khawwāṣ — God's mercy upon him — said: \"The gratitude of the common folk is for food, clothing, and drink; the gratitude of the elite is for the arrivals upon the hearts [of divine gnosis].\" This is a station that not everyone apprehends — those whose pleasures are confined to the belly, the loins, and the sensory perceptions of colors and sounds, and who are devoid of the heart's pleasure. For the heart, in a state of health, takes delight only in the remembrance of God — exalted is He — in knowledge of Him, and in the hope of meeting Him. It only delights in other than Him when it has fallen ill through evil habits — just as some people take pleasure in eating clay, and just as some of the sick find sweet things repugnant and bitter things sweet, as it was said:", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 976", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Gratitude (Shukr) — Its Three Pillars and the Degrees of the Thankful", "section_index": 976, "anchors": "- Root: شكر (Sh-K-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to open, reveal, or fill\"; a beast that thrives on little fodder is called *shakūr* — its fullness of body manifests its owner's care.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Gratitude is \"the conceptualization of the blessing and its manifestation\" (*taṣawwur al-niʿmah wa-iẓhāruhā*). It is the inverse of *kufr* (disbelief/ingratitude), which is forgetting and concealing the blessing. It has three kinds: gratitude of the heart (perceiving the blessing), of the tongue (praising the Benefactor), and of the limbs (requiting the blessing through obedience).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (*ḥaqīqah*): \"I thanked God for His blessing.\" Metaphorical (*majāz*): A beast is called *shakūr* when little fodder suffices it and it fattens — the gratitude is manifest in its condition, not uttered by a tongue.\n- Root: نعم (N-ʿ-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: *Niʿmah* denotes a state of well-being and ease; *naʿīm* is the felicitous life therein.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *Niʿmah* is \"the good state\" (*al-ḥālah al-ḥasanah*), built on the pattern of states like sitting (*jilsah*) and riding (*rikbah*). It applies to both little and much, as in {«وَإِن تَعُدُّوا نِعْمَتَ اللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَا»} (Ibrahim: 34).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"God's blessing and His bounties are immense.\" Metaphorical: \"Their ostriches departed\" (*khafat naʿāmatuhum*) — meaning they fled, a metaphor for the vanishing of comfort.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn a thing over, to rotate it from one face to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"The turning of a thing: directing it and moving it from one aspect to another.\" The heart is so named for its constant fluctuation (*taqallub*).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"I turned the thing over.\" Metaphorical: \"A man *qalb ḥawal*\" — one who turns matters over and devises stratagems; \"they turned the matters over for you\" (Quran 9:48), i.e., they examined and contrived.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (*al-ḥaṣr wa-al-taqyīd bi-innamā*) — The particle *innamā* restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in the author's formulation \"Gratitude is only the acknowledgment of the Benefactor's blessing in a posture of humility\" — a definition that confines the reality to one part (the tongue with a state of the heart) rather than the whole.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "43": [{"arabic": "وقال تعالى {وتلك الأمثال نضربها للناس وما يعقلها إلا العالمون} وقال تعالى {ولو ردوه إلى الرسول وإلى أولي الأمر منهم لعلمه الذين يستنبطونه منهم} رد حكمه في الوقائع إلى استنباطهم وألحق رتبتهم برتبة الأنبياء في كشف حكم الله وقيل في قوله تعالى يا بني آدم قد أنزلنا عليكم لباسا يواري سوءاتكم يعني العلم وريشا يعني اليقين ولباس التقوى يعني الحياء وقال عز وجل {ولقد جئناهم بكتاب فصلناه على علم} وقال تعالى {فلنقصن عليهم بعلم} وقال عز وجل {بل هو آيات بينات في صدور الذين أوتوا العلم} وقال تعالى {خلق الإنسان علمه ال", "english": "How He, Glorified and Exalted, began with Himself, then seconded with the angels, and thirded with the people of knowledge — and sufficient for you is this as honor, excellence, splendor, and nobility. And God, Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, in degrees.\" Ibn ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with them both, said: For the scholars there are degrees above the believers, seven hundred degrees, and between each two degrees is a distance of five hundred years' journey.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 4", "section_title_en": "The Book of Knowledge — Section 4: The Excellence of Knowledge and the Scholars", "section_index": 4, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its true reality; the act of apprehension and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment of existence or non-existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes genuine knowing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to signs, landmarks, and traces that guide to knowledge.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending stations; the root denotes gradation and progressive elevation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a degree is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical steps of ascent. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — lofty rank; \"امش في مدارج الحق\" — walk in the paths of truth.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day denotes the time of the sun's rising to its setting, and may denote any period of time whatsoever.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا\" — historical battles and events; \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — His mighty deeds against the disbelievers.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء»}, where fear of God is restricted exclusively to the scholars.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "45": [{"arabic": "فعلة فيحرم بها قيام سنة وكما إن الصلاة تنهي عن الفحشاء والمنكر فكذلك الفحشاء تنهى عن الصلاة وسائر الخيرات وقال بعض السجانين كنت سجانا نيفا وثلاثين سنة أسأل كل مأخوذ بالليل أنه هل صلى العشاء في جماعة فكانوا يقولون لا وهذا تنبيه على أن بركة الجماعة تنهى عن تعاطي الفحشاء والمنكر وأما الميسرات الباطنة فأربعة أمور الأول سلامة القلب عن الحقد على المسلمين وعن البدع وعن فضول هموم الدنيا فالمستغرق الهم بتدبير الدنيا لا يتيسر له القيام وإن قام فلا يتفكر في صلاته إلا في مهماته ولا يجول إلا في وساوسه وفي مثل ذلك يقال يخبرني البو", "english": "**The fourth:** That one not accumulate sins (awzār) during the day, for sins are among the things that harden the heart and interpose between it and the causes of mercy. A man said to al-Hasan [al-Basrī]: \"O Abū Saʿīd, I spend the night in sound health, I love night prayer, and I prepare my purification water — yet what is the matter that I do not rise [for prayer]?\" He replied: \"Your sins have bound you in chains.\" And al-Hasan — may God have mercy on him — when he entered the marketplace and heard their clamor and idle talk, would say: \"I think that the night of these people is an evil night, for they do not take the midday nap.\"", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 275", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 275: On the Conditions Facilitating Night Prayer (Qiyām al-Layl)", "section_index": 275, "anchors": "- Root: ق-ي-م (Q-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of standing upright, rising, and establishing; Qiyām al-Layl denotes the supererogatory night vigil of prayer and devotion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qiyām carries the sense of \"rising up\" in obedience and \"standing\" in worship, distinguished from mere physical posture by its intentional orientation toward the Divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes physical standing; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to \"rising up\" in resolve, vigilance, and spiritual exertion against the soul's inertia.\n- Root: ن-و-م (N-W-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sleep as the suspension of conscious activity; the root also yields \"manām\" (dream/sleep) and \"nawm\" as a state of repose.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النوم: فسر على أوجه كلها صحيح بنظرات مختلفة\" — Sleep is interpreted in multiple valid ways: as relaxation of the brain's nerves from ascending vapors, as God's \"taking\" of the soul without death {«الله يتوفى الأنفس»}, and as \"a light death\" while death is \"a heavy sleep.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قوم نيام\" (a sleeping people). Metaphorical: \"نامت السوق\" (the market slept = stagnated); \"نام الثوب\" (the garment slept = wore out). Here, sleep's dominance over the body is the literal obstacle, while spiritual heedlessness (ghaflah) is its metaphorical correlate.\n- Root: ق-ل-ب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart as the seat of cognition and moral disposition; the root denotes overturning, turning from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — To turn a thing from one face to another. The heart's \"turning\" (taqlīb) is its oscillation between states of guidance and heedlessness, faith and hardness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الثوب\" (to turn a garment). Metaphorical: \"قلب المعلم الصبيان\" (the teacher turned the boys toward their homes); \"رجل قلب حول\" (a man who turns matters over, discerning). The heart's \"overturning\" (inqilāb) from its prior state of softness to hardness is the precise mechanism of sin's effect described herein.\n- Root: ا-ذ-ا (I-DH-A)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"إذا يعبر به عن كل زمان مستقبل\" — \"Idhā\" denotes any future time, sometimes carrying conditional force. Its use here signals the recurrent, habitual nature of the described causes and effects.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase when the intervening material forms a complete, meaningful unit, as in the conditional structures \"إذا ... فـ\" that organize the causal sequences of this section.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "الرضا فوق جنات عدن كما رفع ذكره فوق الصلاة حيث قال {إن الصلاة تنهي عن الفحشاء والمنكر ولذكر الله أكبر} فكما أن مشاهدة المذكور في الصلاة أكبر من الصلاة فرضوان رب الجنة أعلى من الجنة بل هو غاية مطلب سكان الجنان وفي الحديث إن الله تعالى يتجلى للمؤمنين فيقول سلوني فيقولون رضاك (1) فسؤالهم الرضا بعد النظر نهاية التفضيل وأما رضا العبد فسنذكر حقيقته وأما رضوان الله تعالى عن العبد فهو بمعنى آخر يقرب مما ذكرناه في حب الله للعبد ولا يجوز أن يكشف عن حقيقته إذ تقصر أفهام الخلق عن دركه ومن يقوى عليه فيستقل بإدراكه من نفسه وعلى الجملة فلا رتبة فوق النظر", "english": "I say: As for the verses [indicating the supremacy of divine pleasure], there is the saying of the Exalted: {رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ وَرَضُوا عَنْهُ} [Allah is well-pleased with them and they are well-pleased with Him] {«God is well-pleased with them and they are well-pleased with Him»}. And the Exalted has said: {هَلْ جَزَاءُ الْإِحْسَانِ إِلَّا الْإِحْسَانُ} [Is the reward of goodness aught but goodness?] {«Is the reward of goodness aught but goodness?»}. The ultimate end of goodness is God's pleasure with His servant, and that is the reward for the servant's pleasure in God the Exalted.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1189", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Divine Pleasure (Riḍā) and the Servant's Contentment with the Decree — Section 1189", "section_index": 1189, "anchors": "- Root: رضي (raḍiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to be content, to approve, to be pleased.\" Al-Farahidi notes its usage in mutual accord and satisfaction, with the form *tarāḍin* indicating reciprocal consent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, *riḍā* denotes the will's acquiescence to the divine decree without resistance. It is the apex of the servant's station, being the fruit of *maḥabbah* (love) and the key to perpetual vision (*nazar*).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (*ḥaqīqah*) sense is \"satisfaction of the heart.\" Metaphorical (*majāz*) extension applies to God, where *riḍwān* is not an emotional state but a volitional attribute signifying the bestowal of grace and the removal of the veil.\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To look, to behold, to consider.\" In the lexicon, *naẓar* implies directed vision with the eye or the intellect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Elevated to the highest beatific vision (*ru'yat Allāh*) in the afterlife, the ultimate reward and the supreme object of the inhabitants of Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical sight. Metaphorical: intellectual contemplation or the unveiled witnessing of the Divine in the Hereafter.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التفضيل والتشبيه (The Chapter of Preference and Comparison) — Governing the syntactic structures of *afʿala* (elative) forms like *akbar* (\"greater\") to establish hierarchical supremacy between two entities, as in the verse {وَرِضْوَانٌ مِنَ اللَّهِ أَكْبَرُ}.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "49": [{"arabic": "عليهم بعلم} وقال عز وجل {بل هو آيات بينات في صدور الذين أوتوا العلم} وقال تعالى {خلق الإنسان علمه البيان} وإنما ذكر ذلك في معرض الامتنان وأما الأخبار فقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم من يرد الله به خيرا يفقهه في الدين ويلهمه رشده (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم العلماء ورثة الأنبياء (2) ومعلوم أنه لا رتبة فوق النبوة ولا شرف فوق شرف الوراثة لتلك الرتبة وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يستغفر للعالم ما في السموات والأرض (3) وأي منصب يزيد على منصب من تشتغل ملائكة السموات والأرض بالاستغفار له وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الحكمة تزيد", "english": "How He, Glorified and Exalted, began with Himself, then seconded with the angels, and thirded with the people of knowledge — and sufficient for you is this as honor, excellence, splendor, and nobility. And God, Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, in degrees.\" Ibn ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with them both, said: For the scholars there are degrees above the believers, seven hundred degrees, and between each two degrees is a distance of five hundred years' journey.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 4", "section_title_en": "The Book of Knowledge — Section 4: The Excellence of Knowledge and the Scholars", "section_index": 4, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its true reality; the act of apprehension and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment of existence or non-existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes genuine knowing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to signs, landmarks, and traces that guide to knowledge.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending stations; the root denotes gradation and progressive elevation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a degree is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical steps of ascent. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — lofty rank; \"امش في مدارج الحق\" — walk in the paths of truth.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day denotes the time of the sun's rising to its setting, and may denote any period of time whatsoever.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا\" — historical battles and events; \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — His mighty deeds against the disbelievers.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء»}, where fear of God is restricted exclusively to the scholars.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "52": [{"arabic": "وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يستغفر للعالم ما في السموات والأرض (3) وأي منصب يزيد على منصب من تشتغل ملائكة السموات والأرض بالاستغفار له وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الحكمة تزيد الشريف شرفا وترفع المملوك حتى يدرك مدارك الملوك (4) وقد نبه بهذا على ثمراته في الدنيا ومعلوم أن الآخرة خير وأبقى وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم خصلتان لا يكونان في منافق حسن سمت وفقه في الدين (5) ولا تشكن في الحديث لنفاق بعض فقهاء الزمان فإنه ما أراد به الفقه الذي ظننته وسيأتي معنى الفقه وأدنى درجات الفقيه أن يعلم أن الآخرة خير من الدنيا", "english": "How He, Glorified and Exalted, began with Himself, then seconded with the angels, and thirded with the people of knowledge — and sufficient for you is this as honor, excellence, splendor, and nobility. And God, Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, in degrees.\" Ibn ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with them both, said: For the scholars there are degrees above the believers, seven hundred degrees, and between each two degrees is a distance of five hundred years' journey.", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 4", "section_title_en": "The Book of Knowledge — Section 4: The Excellence of Knowledge and the Scholars", "section_index": 4, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its true reality; the act of apprehension and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment of existence or non-existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes genuine knowing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to signs, landmarks, and traces that guide to knowledge.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending stations; the root denotes gradation and progressive elevation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a degree is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical steps of ascent. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — lofty rank; \"امش في مدارج الحق\" — walk in the paths of truth.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day denotes the time of the sun's rising to its setting, and may denote any period of time whatsoever.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا\" — historical battles and events; \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — His mighty deeds against the disbelievers.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء»}, where fear of God is restricted exclusively to the scholars.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "53": [{"arabic": "عليه شيء مما يسرون وما يعلنون وحذرهم أن يأخذهم بغتة وهم لا يشعرون فقال {ما ينظرون إلا صيحة واحدة تأخذهم وهم يخصمون فلا يستطيعون توصية ولا إلى أهلهم يرجعون} والصلاة والسلام على محمد رسوله الذي يسير تحت لوائه النبيون وعلى آله وأصحابه الأئمة المهديين والسادة المرضيين صلاة يوازي عددها عدد ما كان من خلق الله وما سيكون ويحظى ببركتها الأولون والآخرون وسلم تسليما كثيرا أما بعد فإن الغضب شعلة نار اقتبست من نار الله الموقدة التي تطلع على الأفئدة وإنها لمستكنة في طي الفؤاد استكنان الجمر تحت الرماد ويستخرجه الكبر الدفي", "english": "In the name of God, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. Praise be to God, upon whose pardon and mercy none rely except the hopeful, and from whose evil wrath and assault none fear except the fearful — He who gradually seizes His servants from whence they know not, and who has given dominion over them to their lusts, commanding them to abandon what they desire, and who has tried them with anger while charging them to swallow their rage in what provokes them. Then He surrounded them with hardships and pleasures, and granted them respite to see how they would act. He tested their love through them, to make known the truth of what they claim. He made known to them that nothing is hidden from Him of what they conceal or proclaim, and warned them lest He seize them suddenly while they are unaware. For He said: {«They await but a single blast which will seize them while they are disputing; so they will not be able to make a bequest, nor to their families will they return»} (Q. 36:49-50). And prayers and peace be upon Muhammad, His Messenger, beneath whose banner the prophets walk, and upon his family and companions — the rightly-guided imams and the pleasing masters — prayers whose number equals the number of what has been of God's creation and what will be, and by whose blessing the first and the last are favored — and abundant peace.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 700", "section_title_en": "The Book of Condemnation of Anger, Spite, and Envy — Book Five of the Quarter of Destroyers from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 700, "anchors": "- Root: غضب (ghadab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of anger, rage, fury; the boiling and agitation of the blood\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — when attributed to God, it means retribution (intiqām) alone, not emotional agitation; the Prophet described it as \"a live coal kindled in the heart of the son of Adam\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literal anger is the boiling of the blood in living beings; metaphorical usage extends to inanimate things like fire and the reins of a mount\n- Root: قلب (qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; also the turning, overturning, and transformation of a thing from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — the heart is so named for its constant turning (taqallub) and fluctuation between states; it is the seat of understanding and the locus of divine address\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literal is the physical organ; metaphorical is the subtle spiritual reality (laṭīfah rabbāniyyah) that is the locus of knowledge and will\n- Root: حسد (hasad)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Envy; the wishing for removal of another's blessing\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — envy is the desire for the removal of a blessing from one who deserves it, sometimes accompanied by striving to remove it; the believer envies (ghibṭah) while the hypocrite envies (hasad)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — \"Envy consumes the body as fire consumes wood\"; the envier is one who wishes evil upon the blessed\n- Root: هلك (halak)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perdition, destruction, ruin; death\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — three senses: (1) loss of a thing from one's possession while it exists elsewhere, (2) corruption and dissolution of a thing, (3) death; the Quran uses it for all three\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literal is physical destruction; metaphorical is the ruin of one's religion and afterlife through spiritual corruption\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the intervening material constitutes a complete, meaningful unit, as in the Quranic pattern of extended periodic sentences that delay resolution for rhetorical effect.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "57": [{"arabic": "وقال بعض الصوفية كنت أقرأ ليلة هذه الآية {كل نفس ذائقة الموت} فجعلت أرددها فإذا هاتف يهتف بي كم تردد هذه الآية فقد قتلت أربعة من الجن ما رفعوا رءوسهم إلى السماء منذ خلقوا وقال أبو علي المغازلي للشبلي ربما تطرق سمعي آية من كتاب الله تعالى فتجذبني إلى الإعراض عن الدنيا ثم أرجع إلى أحوالي وإلى الناس فلا أبقى على ذلك فقال ما طرق سمعك من القرآن فاجتذبك به إليه فذلك عطف منه عليك ولطف منه بك وإذا ردك إلى نفسك فهو شفقة منه عليك فإنه لا يصلح لك إلا التبري من الحول والقوة في التوجه إليه وسمع رجل من أه", "english": "Some of the Sufis said: \"One night I was reciting this verse: {كُلُّ نَفْسٍ ذَائِقَةُ الْمَوْتِ} «Every soul shall taste death» [Q. 3:185]. I kept repeating it, and lo, a caller cried out to me: 'How long will you repeat this verse? You have killed four of the jinn who have not raised their heads to the sky since they were created.'\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 512", "section_title_en": "On the Ecstatic States (Wajd) Produced by Qur'anic Recitation and the Superiority of Song (Ghinā') in Stirring the Heart", "section_index": 512, "anchors": "- Root: وجد (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of finding, attaining, and also denoting the emotional state of ecstasy or rapture that \"finds\" the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the theological context, 'wajd' is the state of spiritual finding—a direct, overwhelming apprehension of divine realities that bypasses discursive reasoning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes \"finding\" a lost object; metaphorical (Majaz) usage denotes the heart \"finding\" its spiritual state, leading to agitation and ecstasy.\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty of hearing; also the ear itself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing can be literal (perception of sound) or figurative (understanding, obedience, acceptance). The heart's \"hearing\" is a deeper comprehension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"سمعته\" (I heard him). Metaphorical: \"سمع الله لمن حمده\" (God answers/he accepts the one who praises Him), indicating a responsive, transformative hearing.\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death; the opposite of life.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death is the cessation of the soul's governance over the body, a separation. In the spiritual context, it can be a metaphorical death to worldly desires.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death. Metaphorical: \"أحيا الله البلد الميت\" (God revives the dead land), indicating spiritual revival or the death of the ego.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to emphasize that the stirring of the heart is exclusively through what is appropriate to its state, as in the phrase \"إنما المحرك لما في القلب ما يناسبه\" (The only thing that moves what is in the heart is that which is suitable to it).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "58": [{"arabic": "دونه وادعى الإجماع فيه واستدل بأدلة تشعر بنقيض غرضه كقوله تعالى {الذين آمنوا وعملوا الصالحات} إذ هذا يدل على أن العمل وراء الإيمان لا من نفس الإيمان وإلا فيكون العمل في حكم المعاد والعجب أنه ادعى الإجماع في هذا وهو مع ذلك ينقل قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يكفر أحد إلا بعد جحوده لما أقر به (1) وينكر على المعتزلة قولهم بالتخليد في النار بسبب الكبائر والقائل بهذا قائل بنفس مذهب المعتزلة إذ يقال له من صدق بقلبه وشهد بلسانه ومات في الحال فهل هو في الجنة فلا بد أن يقول نعم وفيه حكم بوجود الإيمان دون العمل فنزيد ونقول لو بقي حيا حتى دخل عليه وقت", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "59": [{"arabic": "تعالى فاصبر كما صبر أولي العزم من الرسل وقال تعالى {نعم أجر العاملين الذين صبروا وعلى ربهم يتوكلون} وهذا في أذى الناس وأما الصبر على أذى الحيات والسباع والعقارب فترك دفعها ليس من التوكل في شيء إذ لا فائدة فيه ولا يراد السعي ولا يترك السعي لعينه بل لإعانته على الدين وترتب الأسباب ههنا كترتبها في الكسب وجلب المنافع فلا نطول بالإعادة وكذلك في الأسباب الدافعة عن المال فلا ينقص التوكل بإغلاق باب البيت عند الخروج ولا بأن يعقل البعير لأن هذه أسباب عرفت سنة الله تعالى إما قطعا وإما ظنا ولذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم للأعرابي لما أن أهمل البعير وقال ت", "english": "...and its end. Those who possess ends are called \"the foremost\" (al-sābiqūn), and those who possess beginnings are called \"the companions of the right\" (aṣḥāb al-yamīn). Then the companions of the right also have degrees, and likewise the foremost; and the highest degrees of the companions of the right adjoin the lowest degrees of the foremost. So there is no meaning to precise estimation in such matters. Rather, the verified truth is that tawakkul (reliance on God) through abandoning provision-storing (tark al-iddikhār) is not perfected except by the shortening of hope (qaṣr al-amal). As for the total absence of hopes for survival, its being stipulated is remote — even in a single soul — for that is as if impossible to exist. As for people, they vary in the length and shortness of hope. The least degree of hope is a day and a night, and what is less than that of hours; and its utmost is what can be conceived to be a man's lifespan. Between these are degrees beyond enumeration. Whoever does not hope for more than a month is closer to the goal than one who hopes for a year. And restricting it to forty [days] on account of the appointed time of Moses (peace be upon him) is far-fetched, for that incident was not intended to clarify the measure of what hope has been permitted. Rather, Moses' worthiness to attain the promised [encounter] was not completed except after forty days, due to a secret by which — and by its like — the sunnah of God most High proceeds in the gradual unfolding of matters, just as he (peace be upon him) said: \"Indeed God kneaded the clay of Adam with His hand for forty mornings\" {«إن الله خمر طينة آدم بيده أربعين صباحا»} — because the worthiness of that clay for kneading was dependent upon a period reaching what was mentioned. Therefore, whatever is beyond a year is not stored except by virtue of the heart's weakness and its leaning toward the outward aspects of causes; such [storing] falls outside the station of tawakkul, [the person] not being confident in the encompassing governance of the True Deputy (al-wakīl al-ḥaqq) over the hidden dimensions of causes. For the means of income — through harvests and alms — recur with the recurrence of years, generally speaking. Whoever stores for less than a year has a degree proportionate to the shortness of his hope; and whoever's hope is two months — his degree is not like the degree of one who hopes for a month, nor the degree of one who hopes for three months; rather, he is between them in rank. Nothing prevents storing except the shortness of hope. So the most virtuous is that one not store at all — even if his heart is weak — for the less his storing, the greater his merit. It has been related concerning the poor man whom the Prophet (peace be upon him) commanded ʿAlī (may God honor his face) and Usāmah to wash — so they washed him and shrouded him in his own cloak. When he buried him, he said to his companions: \"He will be raised on the Day of Resurrection with his face like the full moon on the night of the full moon; and were it not for one trait in him, he would be raised with his face like the radiant sun.\" We said: \"And what is it, O Messenger of God?\" He said: \"He was a frequent faster, a frequent prayer-stancer, and abundant in the remembrance of God most High — except that when winter came, he stored the summer garment for his summer; and when summer came, he stored the winter garment for his winter.\" Then he (peace be upon him) said: \"Rather, the least of what you have been given is certainty (al-yaqīn) and the resolve of patience (ʿazīmat al-ṣabr)\" — the hadith. The jug and the knife and whatever is needed continuously — that is not the meaning of this; for storing such does not diminish the degree. As for the winter garment, it is not needed in summer. And this [censure] applies to one whose heart is not disturbed by abandoning storing, and whose soul does not look longingly toward the hands of creatures; rather, his heart turns to nothing except the True Deputy. But if he senses within himself a restlessness that occupies his heart away from worship, remembrance, and reflection, then storing is more appropriate for him. Indeed, even if he held onto an estate whose income sufficed for his needs, and his heart would not be free except through it, then that is more appropriate for him — because the goal is the rectification of the heart so that it may be emptied for the remembrance of God. There is a person whom the existence of wealth occupies, and a person whom its absence occupies; and what is harmful is whatever occupies one away from God (mighty and majestic). Otherwise, this world in itself is not harmful — neither its existence nor its absence. For this reason, the Messenger of God (peace be upon him) was sent to all classes of creation, among them merchants, craftsmen, and people of trades and industries. He did not command the merchant to abandon his trade, nor the craftsman to abandon his craft, nor did he command the one who had abandoned both to occupy himself with them. Rather, he called everyone to God most High and guided them to the fact that their triumph and salvation lie in the turning of their hearts away from this world toward God most High. The pillar of occupation with God (mighty and majestic) is the heart. So the correct course for the weak is to store the measure of his need, just as the correct course for the strong is to abandon storing.", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1132", "section_title_en": "On Reliance (Tawakkul): Degrees of Hope, Provision-Storing (Iddikhār), and the Means of Repelling Harm", "section_index": 1132, "anchors": "- Root: أمل (ʾ-m-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Prolonged expectation or hoping for something; the extension of one's aspiration toward a future state.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope (amal) is the soul's expectation of a beloved matter; its opposite is yaʾs (despair). In the Kalam context, it is tied to the length of one's expectation of worldly continuance, which directly governs the validity of tawakkul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) usage denotes the temporal span of expectation; metaphorical (majāz) usage applies to the soul's attachment to causes. Here, Ghazali uses it literally to measure degrees of trust.\n- Root: ذكر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remember, to mention; the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Dhikr is the presence of the remembered thing in the heart; its highest form is absorption in the remembrance of God, which is the very purpose of heart-rectification (iṣlāḥ al-qalb).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: verbal mention; metaphorical: the heart's unwavering orientation toward the object of remembrance, as in \"the heart's distraction from worship and dhikr.\"\n- Root: سبب (s-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A rope by which one climbs; a means or connection to something.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Everything by which one reaches something else is called a sabab. God has ordained causes (asbāb) as the customary channel (sunnah) of His decree; reliance is placed not on the cause itself but on the Causer of causes (musabbib al-asbāb).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a physical cord or link; metaphorical: any means, whether certain (maqṭūʿ), probable (maẓnūn), or illusory (mawhūm). Ghazali's tripartite division of causes rests on this rhetorical distinction.\n- Root: وكل (w-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To entrust, to delegate; one who relies upon another as a deputy.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tawakkul is to depend upon another and make him a deputy over one's affair. The true wakīl is God alone, for He alone encompasses the hidden dimensions of causes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delegation of authority; metaphorical: the heart's utter confidence in the deputy's sufficiency, as in \"he entrusted his burden to God.\" Ghazali contrasts the strong (qawī) who abandons storing with the weak (ḍaʿīf) who stores out of necessity.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — Used to delimit the conditions of tawakkul, e.g., \"the one who stores is not excluded from tawakkul except by the length of his hope,\" and to distinguish what nullifies reliance from what does not.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "60": [{"arabic": "برحنا ولا قمنا حتى نزلت {وكأين من دابة لا تحمل رزقها الله يرزقها وإياكم وهو السميع العليم} قال فقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الله لم يأمركم بكنز المال ولا بإتباع الشهوات من كنز دنانير يريد بها حياة فانية فإن الحياة بيد الله ألا وإني لا أكنز دينارا ولا درهما ولا أخبأ رزقا لغد // حديث ابن عمر خرجت مع رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم حتى دخل على حيطان الأنصار فجعل يلتقط من التمر ويأكل الحديث أخرجه ابن مردويه في التفسير والبيهقي في الزهد من رواية رجل لم يسم عن ابن عمر قال البيهقي هذا إسناد مجهول والجراح بن منهال ضعيف وقال أبو الدر", "english": "And he — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — said: \"Never did Gabriel come to me except that he was trembling, in fear of the Almighty\" (3).", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 1059", "section_title_en": "The Fear of God and the Weeping of the Prophets — On the Terror of the Angels and the Lamentation of David (Peace Be Upon Him)", "section_index": 1059, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (wajila)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to fear, to be apprehensive, to tremble inwardly.\" Al-Farahidi notes its intensity — a trembling that seizes the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, *wajal* is the fear born of awe before divine majesty (*haybah*), distinct from mere dread of punishment; it is the trembling of the heart at the threshold of the Sacred.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (*haqiqah*): physical trembling. Metaphorical (*majaz*): the soul's quaking before divine power — as in the *azīz* (boiling) of the breast during prayer, a sound likened to a cauldron.\n- Root: بكى (baka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To weep, to shed tears.\" Ibn Manzur records the intensive forms *bakkā'* (much-weeping) and the noun *bukā'* as the outward expression of inward grief (*ḥuzn*).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Weeping is the physical sign of the heart's brokenness (*inkisār*); in the prophets, it is the overflow of *khashyah* — reverent fear — not mere sorrow.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: tears flowing. Metaphorical: the \"weeping\" of the sky (rain) and the \"weeping\" of the heart (contrition) — as in David's lament, where tears substitute for speech.\n- Root: خطأ (khaṭi'a)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To err, to commit a fault.\" Al-Farahidi distinguishes *khaṭī'ah* (a sin committed unintentionally or through weakness) from *ithm* (deliberate transgression).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, *khaṭī'ah* for a prophet is a *tark al-awlā* — the abandonment of the more excellent course — not moral depravity; its gravity lies in the station of the one who errs.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: missing the mark. Metaphorical: the soul's deviation from the straight path of intimacy (*uns*) with God, producing *waḥshah* (estrangement).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated conditional and vocative structures (*yā Dāwūd*, *idhā dhakartu khaṭī'atī*) serve to restrict the predicate to the speaker's subjective state, emphasizing the direct, unmediated dialogue between the prophet and his Lord.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "61": [{"arabic": "ولذلك قال عز وجل ولئن سألتهم من خلق السموات والأرض ليقولن الله وقال تعالى فأقم وجهك للدين حنيفا فطرة الله التي", "english": "The First Principle: the knowledge of His existence, the Exalted. The first thing by which one is illuminated from among the lights, and the path of reflection (ṭarīq al-iʿtibār) by which one proceeds, is that to which the Qurʾān has guided. For after the explanation of God, glorified be He, there is no explanation. He, the Exalted, has said: {«أَلَمْ نَجْعَلِ الْأَرْضَ مِهَادًا وَالْجِبَالَ أَوْتَادًا وَخَلَقْنَاكُمْ أَزْوَاجًا وَجَعَلْنَا نَوْمَكُمْ سُبَاتًا وَجَعَلْنَا اللَّيْلَ لِبَاسًا وَجَعَلْنَا النَّهَارَ مَعَاشًا وَبَنَيْنَا فَوْقَكُمْ سَبْعًا شِدَادًا وَجَعَلْنَا سِرَاجًا وَهَّاجًا وَأَنزَلْنَا مِنَ الْمُعْصِرَاتِ مَاءً ثَجَّاجًا لِّنُخْرِجَ بِهِ حَبًّا وَنَبَاتًا وَجَنَّاتٍ أَلْفَافًا»} — \"Have We not made the earth a resting place, and the mountains as pegs, and created you in pairs, and made your sleep a repose, and made the night a covering, and made the day for livelihood, and built above you seven strong heavens, and made a blazing lamp, and sent down from the rain-clouds pouring water, that We may bring forth thereby grain and vegetation, and gardens dense with foliage?\" (Q 78:6–16). And He, the Exalted, has said: {«إِنَّ فِي خَلْقِ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ وَاخْتِلَافِ اللَّيْلِ وَالنَّهَارِ وَالْفُلْكِ الَّتِي تَجْرِي فِي الْبَحْرِ بِمَا يَنفَعُ النَّاسَ وَمَا أَنزَلَ اللَّهُ مِنَ السَّمَاءِ مِن مَّاءٍ فَأَحْيَا بِهِ الْأَرْضَ بَعْدَ مَوْتِهَا وَبَثَّ فِيهَا مِن كُلِّ دَابَّةٍ وَتَصْرِيفِ الرِّيَاحِ وَالسَّحَابِ الْمُسَخَّرِ بَيْنَ السَّمَاءِ وَالْأَرْضِ لَآيَاتٍ لِّقَوْمٍ يَعْقِلُونَ»} — \"Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth, and the alternation of the night and the day, and the ships which sail through the sea with that which benefits people, and what God has sent down from the sky of water, thereby reviving the earth after its death, and dispersing therein every creature, and the directing of the winds, and the clouds subservient between the heaven and the earth — are signs for a people who reason\" (Q 2:164). And He, the Exalted, has said: {«أَلَمْ تَرَوْا كَيْفَ خَلَقَ اللَّهُ سَبْعَ سَمَاوَاتٍ طِبَاقًا وَجَعَلَ الْقَمَرَ فِيهِنَّ نُورًا وَجَعَلَ الشَّمْسَ سِرَاجًا وَاللَّهُ أَنبَتَكُم مِّنَ الْأَرْضِ نَبَاتًا ثُمَّ يُعِيدُكُمْ فِيهَا وَيُخْرِجُكُمْ إِخْرَاجًا»} — \"Have you not seen how God created seven heavens in layers, and made the moon therein a light, and made the sun a lamp? And God caused you to grow from the earth a growth; then He returns you into it, and brings you forth a bringing forth\" (Q 71:15–18). And He, the Exalted, has said: {«أَفَرَأَيْتُم مَّا تُمْنُونَ أَأَنتُمْ تَخْلُقُونَهُ أَمْ نَحْنُ الْخَالِقُونَ»} — \"Have you considered what you emit? Do you create it, or are We the Creator?\" — up to His saying: {«لِلْمُقْوِينَ»} — \"for the settlers\" (Q 56:58–73).", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 87", "section_title_en": "The Pillars of Faith: Knowledge of God, His Attributes, His Acts, and the Audibles — Section 87", "section_index": 87, "anchors": "- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: to find, to perceive, to be; existence as that which is found or discovered]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: al-wujūd as necessary existence (wujūb) versus contingent existence (imkān); the self-evident reality of the Creator's being]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: haqīqah — existence as affirmed reality; majāz — \"finding\" as in discovery, not applicable to God's necessary being]\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: measuring, proportioning, fashioning; origination of a thing upon a measure]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم، ويستعمل في إبداع الشيء من غير أصل ولا احتذاء\" — creation as origination from nothing without prior model\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal: خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة — brought into existence upon a measure necessitated by wisdom; Majaz: not applicable to God's act as pure origination]\n- Root: فطر (f-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: to split, cleave, rend; to originate, to bring forth as a new creation]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"فطر الله الخلق\" — God's origination of creation as a primordial act; \"الفطرة\" as the innate disposition\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal: فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها — originator without precedent; Majaz: \"رأيه فطير\" — immature opinion, not applicable to divine]\n- Root: ارض (ar-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: the earth, the lower sphere; that which is beneath]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء\" — the body opposite the heaven\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal: the earth as a resting place (مهادا); Majaz: not applicable in this context of divine creation]\n- Root: ركن (r-k-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: corner, pillar, support; that to which one leans for strength]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ركن الشيء: جانبه الذي يسكن إليه\" — the side of a thing to which one repairs; used metaphorically for strength\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal: physical pillar/corner; Majaz: \"فلان يأوي من عز قومه إلى ركن شديد\" — one who repairs to a mighty support; here, the \"pillars\" (أركان) of faith as structural supports]\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic structure \"مداره على عشرة أصول\" (its circuit revolves upon ten principles) employs a prepositional phrase governing the verbal noun, establishing the necessary grammatical dependency that mirrors the ontological dependency of the created upon the Creator.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "64": [{"arabic": "بنفسك اليوم عليك حسيبا} ومعنى قوله تعالى {وإن الدار الآخرة لهي الحيوان لو كانوا يعلمون} ومعنى لقاء الله عز وجل والنظر إلى وجهه الكريم ومعنى القرب منه والنزول في جواره ومعنى حصول السعادة بمرافقة الملأ الأعلى ومقارنة الملائكة والنبيين ومعنى تفاوت درجات أهل الجنان حتى يرى بعضهم البعض كما يرى الكوكب الدري في جوف السماء إلى غير ذلك مما يطول تفصيله إذ للناس في معاني هذه الأمور بعد التصديق بأصولها مقامات شتى فبعضهم يرى أن جميع ذلك أمثلة وأن الذي أعده الله لعباده الصالحين ما لا عين رأت ولا أذن سمعت ولا خطر على قلب بشر وأنه ليس مع الخلق من", "english": "He, exalted, said: {«لهم قلوب لا يفقهون بها»} [They have hearts with which they do not understand] (Q 7:179), meaning thereby the realities of faith, not legal fatwas. By my life, jurisprudence and understanding (fahm) are in language two names with one meaning; the discussion concerns customary usage, both ancient and modern.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 16", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 16: The Science of Unveiling (ʿIlm al-Mukāshafah) and the Science of Practical Conduct (ʿIlm al-Muʿāmalah)", "section_index": 16, "anchors": "- Root: كشف (kashafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Removal of a covering; unveiling, disclosure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The lifting of a veil (ghishāwah) from the heart such that the realities of things become manifest as if witnessed directly (muʿāyanah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): removal of a physical covering. Metaphorical (majāz): the illumination of the heart with the light of certainty, dispelling the darkness of doubt and heedlessness.\n- Root: عرف (ʿarafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To know by reflection and trace; to recognize.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Maʿrifah\" is the perception of a thing through reflection upon its effects; it is more specific than ʿilm and is the knowledge of God attained by the servants through His signs, not by grasping His essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to recognize a person or thing. Metaphorical: the knowledge of God that necessitates reverential fear and love, as opposed to mere information.\n- Root: عمل (ʿamila)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To do, to act, to perform.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The act (ʿamal) is the movement of the servant toward a goal; in religious usage, it is the movement of the heart and limbs in obedience or disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical action. Metaphorical: the actions of the heart (aʿmāl al-qulūb), which are the inward realities of faith.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The distinction between the two sciences is a restriction of the term \"science\" (ʿilm) to its two true referents: the science of unveiling (mukāshafah) and the science of practical conduct (muʿāmalah), excluding all else that is falsely named science.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "65": [{"arabic": "الحواريون نبيهم فقيل لهم توجه نحو البحر فانطلقوا يطلبونه فلما انتهو إلى البحر إذا هو قد أقبل يمشى على الماء فذكر حديثا فيه أن عيسى قال لو أن لابن آدم من اليقين شعره مشى على الماء وروى أبو منصور الديلمى فى مسند الفردوس بسند ضعيف من حديث معاذ بن جبل لو عرفتم الله حق معرفته لمشيتم على البحور ولزالت بدعائكم الجبال فهذه رموز وإشارات إلى معنى الكراهة والمحبة والرضا والغضب والشكر والكفران لا يليق بعلم المعاملة أكثر منها وقد ضرب الله تعالى مثلا لذلك تقريبا إلى أفهام الخلق إذ عرف أنه ما خلق الجن والإنس إلا ليعبدوه فكانت عبادتهم غاية ال", "english": "Thus was the beginning of this matter and its end. And do not understand it unless you are worthy of it. And if you are worthy of it, the eye is opened and you see, so you have no need of a leader to lead you. The blind man can indeed be led, but only to a limit. For when the path narrows and becomes sharper than a sword and finer than a hair, the bird is able to fly over it but is not able to drag a blind man behind it. And when the field becomes narrow and subtle — subtle as water, for example — and crossing is possible only by swimming, then the one skilled in the art of swimming may be able to cross by himself, and perhaps he is not able to drag another behind him. These matters bear the same relation to walking upon the way of the common masses of creatures as walking upon water bears to walking upon earth. Swimming can be learned; but as for walking upon water, it is not acquired by instruction, but rather is attained through the strength of certainty (yaqīn). For this reason it was said to the Prophet, peace be upon him, that Jesus, peace be upon him, was said to have walked upon water, and he, peace be upon him, said: \"Had his certainty increased, he would have walked upon the air.\" {«حديث قيل له يقال إن عيسى مشى على الماء قال لو ازداد يقينا لمشي على الهواء»} — This hadith is rejected (munkar) and is not known in this form. What is known is what Ibn Abi al-Dunya narrated in the Book of Certainty from the saying of Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, who said: The disciples lost their Prophet, and it was said to them: \"He has headed toward the sea.\" So they set out seeking him, and when they reached the sea, behold, he was coming toward them walking upon the water. And he mentioned a hadith in which Jesus said: \"If the son of Adam had a hair's breadth of certainty, he would walk upon the water.\" And Abu Mansur al-Daylami narrated in the Musnad al-Firdaws with a weak chain from the hadith of Muadh ibn Jabal: \"If you knew God with true knowledge, you would walk upon the seas, and the mountains would be removed by your supplications.\" These are symbols and allusions to the meaning of aversion (karāhah) and love (maḥabbah), and good pleasure (riḍā) and wrath (ghaḍab), and gratitude (shukr) and ingratitude (kufrān) — more than this is not fitting for the science of practical conduct (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah). And God, exalted be He, struck an example of this to draw it near to the understanding of creatures, since He knew that He created the jinn and mankind only to worship Him, and their worship was the ultimate purpose of wisdom (ghāyat al-ḥikmah) with respect to them. Then He informed that He has two servants: one whom He loves, whose name is Gabriel — the Holy Spirit and the Trustworthy One — and he is with Him beloved, obeyed, trustworthy, and honored; and another whom He hates, whose name is Iblis — the accursed one, reprieved until the Day of Judgment. Then He delegated guidance (irshād) to Gabriel, saying, the Exalted: {«قل نزله روح القدس من ربك بالحق»} \"Say: The Holy Spirit has brought it down from your Lord with truth\" (16:102), and said, the Exalted: {«يلقي الروح من أمره على من يشاء من عباده»} \"He casts the Spirit of His command upon whom He wills of His servants\" (40:15). And He delegated misguidance (ighwāʾ) to Iblis, saying, the Exalted: {«ليضل عن سبيله»} \"That he may lead astray from His path\" (4:119). And ighwāʾ is the halting of servants short of attaining the ultimate purpose of wisdom. So observe how He attributed it to the servant with whom He was wrathful; and guidance (irshād) is the conduct of them toward the ultimate purpose — so observe how He attributed it to the servant whom He loves. And you have an example of this in custom: when a king is in need of one to give him drink and one to cup him and to clean the courtyard of his house of filth, and he has two servants, he does not appoint for cupping and cleaning except the ugliest and meanest of them, and he does not entrust the carrying of drink and perfume except to the best and most complete of them and the most beloved to him. And you should not say: \"This is my act, and his act was not without my act\" — for you have erred when you attributed that to yourself. Rather, He is the One who directed your motive (dāʿiyah) to the specification of the disliked act to the disliked person, and the beloved act to the beloved person, as a completion of justice (ʿadl). For His justice is sometimes completed through matters in which you have no entry, and sometimes it is completed in you — for you also are among His acts. So your motive, your power, your knowledge, your action, and all the other causes of your movements in expression — all of this is His act, which He arranged with justice in an arrangement from which moderate acts issue forth. Except that you see only yourself, and so you suppose that what appears through you in the world of witness (ʿālam al-shahādah) has no cause from the world of the unseen and the dominion (ʿālam al-ghayb wa al-malakūt). Therefore you attribute it to yourself. And you are only like the child who looks at night at the play of the juggler (mushʿabadh) who brings forth figures from behind a curtain that dance and scream and rise and sit, composed of rags that do not move by themselves — rather, they are moved by fine hair-like threads that do not appear in the darkness of night, their ends in the hand of the juggler, who is hidden from the eyes of the children. So they rejoice and marvel, supposing that those rags dance and play and rise and sit. As for the intelligent ones, they know that this is a moving (taḥrīk) and not a self-movement, but perhaps they do not know its detailed modality. And the one who knows some of its detail does not know it as the juggler knows it, to whom the command belongs and in whose hand is the pulling. Likewise, the children of the people of this world — and all creatures are children relative to the scholars — look at these persons (ashkhāṣ) and suppose that they are the movers, and so they attribute [action] to them. And the scholars know that they are moved, except that they do not know the modality of the moving — and they are the majority. Except the knowers (ʿārifūn), the scholars, and those firmly rooted in knowledge (al-rāsikhūn), for they have perceived, with the sharpness of their sight, fine spider-web threads — nay, much finer than these — suspended from heaven, their ends attached to the persons of the people of the earth. Those threads are not perceived, due to their fineness, by these outward eyes. Then they witnessed the heads of those threads in their suspension-points (manāṭāt) to which they are attached, and they witnessed for those suspension-points handles (maqābiḍ) that are in the hands of the angels who move the heavens. And they also witnessed the angels of the heavens directed toward the bearers of the Throne (ḥamalat al-ʿarsh), awaiting what descends upon them of the command from the presence of Lordship (ḥaḍrat al-rubūbiyyah) — \"they do not disobey God in what He commands them, and they do what they are commanded\" (66:6). And He expressed these witnessed realities in the Qur'an, and it was said: {«وفي السماء رزقكم وما توعدون»} \"And in the heaven is your provision and what you are promised\" (51:22). And He expressed the waiting of the angels of the heavens for what descends upon them of decree and command, and it was said: \"He created seven heavens and of the earth the like of them; {«يتنزل الأمر بينهن لتعلموا أن الله على كل شيء قدير وأن الله قد أحاط بكل شيء علما»} 'the command descends between them, that you may know that God is powerful over all things and that God encompasses all things in knowledge'\" (65:12). These are matters whose interpretation (taʾwīl) none knows except God and those firmly rooted in knowledge (al-rāsikhūn fī al-ʿilm). And Ibn Abbas, may God be pleased with them both, expressed the exclusivity of those firmly rooted in knowledge to sciences that the understandings of creatures cannot bear, when he recited the saying of the Exalted: {«يتنزل الأمر بينهن»} \"The command descends between them,\" and said: \"Were I to mention what I know of the meaning of this verse, you would stone me\" — and in another wording: \"You would say he is an unbeliever.\"", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 990", "section_title_en": "On the Degrees of Proximity to God and the Hierarchy of Those Who Perfect Divine Wisdom", "section_index": 990, "anchors": "- Root: سير (s-y-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"السير: الذهاب والسفر، وسار يسير سيرا ومسيرا، والسيارة: الرفقة المسافرة\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السير: الانتقال من مكان إلى مكان، ويستعار للسلوك في الدين والطريقة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"سار القوم: مشوا\" — [Metaphorical] \"سار فيه السير: مضى في الأمر، وسار ذكره: انتشر، ومنه: سيرة فلان: طريقته\"\n- Root: مشي (m-sh-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المشي: تردد الأركان على الأرض، والماشية: الإبل والغنم لانتقالها في المرعى\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"المشي: الانتقال من مكان إلى مكان بإرادة، ويكنى به عن النميمة، وعن شرب المسهل\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"مشيت وتمشيت، وهي حسنة المشية\" — [Metaphorical] \"مشى بطنه، وأمشاه الدواء، واستمشيت بالدواء، ومنه: مشت المرأة: كثرت أولادها\"\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخلق: التقدير، والخلق: الناس، والخليقة: الطبيعة، والخلق: السجية\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم، ويستعمل في إبداع الشيء من غير أصل ولا احتذاء\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"خلق الخراز الأديم: قدره قبل القطع\" — [Metaphorical] \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\"\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النفس: الروح، والذات، والشيء النفيس\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح، وتطلق على الذات، قال تعالى: {ويحذركم الله نفسه} فنفسه: ذاته\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — [Metaphorical] \"دفق نفسه أي دمه، وأصابته نفس: عين\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish exclusive attribution of actions to God versus creatures, as in \"إنما أنت مثل الصبي\" (You are only like a child), restricting the simile to the creature while preserving divine agency.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وجهل بحقائق الأمور ولذلك قال تعالى {فإذا ركبوا في الفلك دعوا الله مخلصين له الدين فلما نجاهم إلى البر إذا هم يشركون} قيل معناه أنهم يقولون لولا استواء الريح لما نجونا ومن انكشف له أمر العالم كما هو عليه علم أن الريح هو الهواء والهواء لا يتحرك بنفسه مالم يحركه محرك وكذلك محركه وهكذا إلى أن ينتهي إلى المحرك الأول الذي لا محرك له ولا هو متحرك في نفسه عز وجل فالتفات العبد في النجاة إلى الريح يضاهي التفات من أخذ لتحزرقبته كتب الملك توقيعا بالعفو عنه وتخليته فأخذ يشتغل بذكر الحبر والكاغد والقلم الذي به يكتب التوقيع يقول لولا القلم لما تخلصت فيرى نجاته من القلم لا م", "english": "This witnessing (mushāhadah), in which nothing appears but the One Real (al-Wāḥid al-Ḥaqq), sometimes endures and sometimes flashes suddenly like a lightning bolt — and the latter is the more frequent. Perpetuity is rare and precious. To this alluded al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr al-Ḥallāj when he saw al-Khawwāṣ journeying through the lands and said to him: \"In what are you engaged?\" He replied: \"I journey through the lands to rectify my state in trust in God (tawakkul)\" — and he had been among those who trust [in God]. Al-Ḥusayn said: \"You have spent your life in cultivating your outward self — so where is annihilation (fanāʾ) in unity (tawḥīd)?\" It was as though al-Khawwāṣ had been engaged in rectifying the third station in unity, and so he demanded of him the fourth station. These, then, are the stations of the monotheists (muwaḥḥidūn) in unity, in summary fashion.", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1106", "section_title_en": "The Stations of the Monotheists (Maqāmāt al-Muwaḥḥidīn) — On the Unfolding of Unity and Its Bearing on Trust in God", "section_index": 1106, "anchors": "- Root: وحد (w-ḥ-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core sense of singularity and indivisibility; al-wāḥid as that which has no part whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-waḥdah is \"al-infirād\" (exclusive solitude); the One in truth is that which admits no composition, used across six nuanced senses from generic unity to existential uniqueness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes numerical and essential oneness; metaphorical (majāz) extension applies to acts of exclusive agency, as in \"He alone is the Agent.\"\n- Root: وكل (w-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Entrusting, deputizing; al-tawakkul as reliance upon another as deputy.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-tawkīl: that you depend upon another and make him your representative\"; al-Wakīl is the One who undertakes your affair — God alone, without peer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delegation of authority; metaphorical: the soul's utter dependence, as in \"tawakkala ʿalā Allāh\" — contrasted with weak \"takkala\" (leaning on creatures).\n- Root: قلم (q-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Trimming/cutting of a hard substance; then the writing reed; also the arrow shaft.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The pen as instrument of inscription; in Quranic usage {«عَلَّمَ بِالْقَلَمِ»} it signals divine teaching through means — yet the means has no independent power.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the reed; metaphorical: the pen as a mere tool, utterly subjugated to the writer's hand — a paradigm for all creatures under divine power.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and exclusive predication (al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the assertion that agency belongs to God alone, negating it from all others, as in \"lā fāʿila illā Allāh.\"", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "68": [{"arabic": "الله تعالى بين من افترى على الله كذبا وبين من كذب بالحق فقال تعالى ومن أظلم ممن افترى على الله كذبا أو كذب بالحق لما جاءه وقال تعالى فمن أظلم ممن كذب على الله وكذب بالصدق إذ جاءه ومنها الرياء وملاحظة الخلق والجهد في استمالة قلوبهم وصرف وجوههم والرياء هو الداء العضال الذي يدعو إلى أكبر الكبائر كما سيأتي في كتاب الرياء والمناظر لا يقصد إلا الظهور عند الخلق وانطلاق ألسنتهم بالثناء عليه فهذه عشر خصال من أمهات الفواحش الباطنة سوى ما يتفق لغير المتماسكين منهم من الخصام المؤدي إلى الضرب", "english": "**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to look, to examine, to consider; al-munāẓarah is mutual examination/argumentation between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: naẓar as intellectual contemplation (taʾammul bi-l-baṣīrah); munāẓarah as dialectical exchange.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sitting face-to-face in debate; Metaphorical: disputation for victory.\n- Root: غلب (ghalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to overcome, to conquer, to prevail.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghalabah as seeking to dominate another in argument.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: victory in combat; Metaphorical: intellectual supremacy.\n- Root: حسد (ḥasada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to envy, to wish removal of blessings from another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: envy as wishing deprivation of blessings from the deserving — among the basest traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: envy of another's blessing; Metaphorical: consuming fire of the soul.\n- Root: كبر (kabura)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be great, to be haughty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-kibr as self-elevation above others; condemned by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: greatness; Metaphorical: arrogance and pride.\n- Root: غيب (ghāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be absent, to be hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghībah as mentioning another with what he dislikes in his absence — likened to eating carrion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: backbiting; Metaphorical: consuming the flesh of one's brother.\n- Root: راءى (rāʾā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to show off, to display.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as performing acts for the sake of being seen — hidden shirk.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: showing good deeds; Metaphorical: seeking reputation.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: self, soul, spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: tazkiyat al-nafs as self-praise and self-purification — forbidden by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: self; Metaphorical: self-commendation.\n- Root: حقd (ḥaqida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to bear malice, to harbor resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ḥiqd as nursing enmity in the heart — negated from the believer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bearing grudge; Metaphorical: hidden rancor.\n- Root: نفاق (nifāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: hypocrisy, showing contrary to what one conceals.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: hypocrisy in deeds — outward display contrary to inward state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hypocrisy in faith; Metaphorical: hypocrisy in character.\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to know, to perceive.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: knowledge as apprehension of reality — its pursuit for other than God is destructive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge; Metaphorical: the dangerous burden of the scholar.\n- Root: جدل (jadala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to argue, to dispute.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-jidāl as contention; al-mirāʾ as disputation with falsehood — condemned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: argumentation; Metaphorical: obstinate disputation.\n- Root: شرك (sharaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to associate, to share.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as hidden shirk — associating others with God in intention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: polytheism; Metaphorical: hidden association in deeds.\n- Root: موت (māta)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to die.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: death as separation of soul from body; spiritual death as ruin of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; Metaphorical: spiritual destruction.\n- Root: حيى (ḥayiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to live.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: life as the power of perception; eternal life as the reward of the righteous.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical life; Metaphorical: spiritual vitality.\n- Root: هلك (halaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to perish, to be destroyed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-halāk as ruin; eternal destruction as everlasting punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: destruction; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: رياء (riyāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: showing off, ostentation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: riyāʾ as the incurable disease calling to the greatest sins.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ostentation; Metaphorical: spiritual disease.\n- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: rank, status, prestige.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of status (ḥubb al-jāh) among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: position; Metaphorical: seeking prestige.\n- Root: مباهاة (mubāhāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mutual boasting, vying in splendor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: mubāhāh as each seeking to display superiority over the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mutual boasting; Metaphorical: rivalry in honor.\n- Root: مماراة (mumārāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: disputation, wrangling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mirāʾ as contentious argument — condemned by the Prophet.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: disputation; Metaphorical: obstinate wrangling.\n- Root: فخر (fakhara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to boast, to pride oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fakhr as self-glorification — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boasting; Metaphorical: vainglory.\n- Root: عجب (ʿajiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to wonder, to be amazed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿujb as self-admiration — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder; Metaphorical: self-conceit.\n- Root: بغض (baghiḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to hate, to detest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baghdāʾ as mutual hatred — contrary to brotherhood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hatred; Metaphorical: mutual enmity.\n- Root: غضب (ghaḍiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be angry.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaḍab as anger — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; Metaphorical: rage.\n- Root: طمع (ṭamiʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to covet, to desire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ṭamaʿ as covetousness — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: desire; Metaphorical: greed.\n- Root: أشر (ashira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be exultant, to be insolent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ashar as excessive joy and insolence — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: exultation; Metaphorical: insolence.\n- Root: بطر (baṭira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be arrogant, to be ungrateful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baṭar as arrogance in prosperity — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arrogance; Metaphorical: ingratitude for blessings.\n- Root: خيلاء (khuyalāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: pride, haughtiness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khuyalāʾ as pride and conceit — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pride; Metaphorical: haughty bearing.\n- Root: خشية (khashyah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fear, awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khashyah as reverential fear — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fear; Metaphorical: reverential awe.\n- Root: رحمة (raḥmah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mercy, compassion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-raḥmah as tenderness of heart — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mercy; Metaphorical: compassion.\n- Root: غفلة (ghaflah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: heedlessness, inattention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaflah as heedlessness — contrary to vigilance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inattention; Metaphorical: spiritual heedlessness.\n- Root: خشوع (khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: humility, submissiveness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khushūʿ as humility — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: humility; Metaphorical: devotional submission.\n- Root: تذكير (tadhkīr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reminding, admonishing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-tadhkīr as reminding of God — a righteous act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reminding; Metaphorical: preaching.\n- Root: وعظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to admonish, to exhort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waʿẓ as exhortation to obedience and warning against disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: admonition; Metaphorical: sermonizing.\n- Root: فتوى (fatwā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: legal ruling, formal opinion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fatwā as clarification of legal rulings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: legal opinion; Metaphorical: religious authority.\n- Root: قضاء (qaḍāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: judgment, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-qaḍāʾ as judgment between people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: judgment; Metaphorical: judicial authority.\n- Root: وقف (waqafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to stop, to stand.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waqf as endowment of property for charitable purposes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing; Metaphorical: religious endowment.\n- Root: ثواب (thawāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reward, recompense.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-thawāb as divine recompense for righteous deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reward; Metaphorical: divine recompense.\n- Root: عذاب (ʿadhāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: punishment, torment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿadhāb as divine punishment for the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: punishment; Metaphorical: divine chastisement.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to possess, to rule.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mulk as sovereignty; al-mulk al-muʾabbad as eternal sovereignty in Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dominion; Metaphorical: eternal kingdom.\n- Root: نعيم (naʿīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: bliss, delight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-naʿīm as bliss; al-naʿīm al-sarmad as everlasting bliss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delight; Metaphorical: eternal bliss.\n- Root: رخصة (rukhaṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: concession, dispensation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rukhaṣah as legal concession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: concession; Metaphorical: legal dispensation.\n- Root: رياسة (riyāsah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: leadership, headship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of leadership among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: leadership; Metaphorical: seeking authority.\n- Root: اندراس (indirās)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: effacement, obliteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: indirās al-ʿulūm as the obliteration of the sciences.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: effacement; Metaphorical: extinction of knowledge.\n- Root: صبيان (ṣibyān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: boys, children.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: children's motivation for learning through play.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boys; Metaphorical: pupils.\n- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: place of instruction, school for boys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the elementary school where children are taught.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: school; Metaphorical: place of elementary learning.\n- Root: خلاق (khilāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: portion, share, lot.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"no portion\" (lā khilāq) in the Hereafter — utter deprivation of divine reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: share; Metaphorical: spiritual allotment in the afterlife.\n- Root: فاجر (fājir)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: wicked, immoral, profligate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the dissolute one who commits sins openly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sinner; Metaphorical: one through whom God nonetheless supports religion.\n- Root: شمع (shamʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: candle, wax taper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the candle as parable — consuming itself while giving light to others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: candle; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself but benefits others.\n- Root: نار (nār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fire as parable — consuming itself and all around it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fire; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself and others.\n- Root: سعد (saʿida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be fortunate, to prosper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: saʿādah as felicity — success in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: good fortune; Metaphorical: eternal felicity.\n- Root: شقي (shaqiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be wretched, to be miserable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: shaqāwah as wretchedness — ruin in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: misery; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: أخلص (akhlaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to purify, to be sincere.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: ikhlāṣ as purifying intention for God alone — the condition of acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sincerity; Metaphorical: pure devotion.\n- Root: ريب (rāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to doubt, to suspect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rayb as doubt and uncertainty — to be dispelled by evidence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: doubt; Metaphorical: skepticism.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 39", "section_title_en": "Section 39", "section_index": 39, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "69": [{"arabic": "بالمجاهدة التي جعلها الله سبحانه مقدمة للهداية حيث قال تعالى {والذين جاهدوا فينا لنهدينهم سبلنا وإن الله لمع المحسنين} فإن قلت فقد رددت حد المتكلم إلى حراسة عقيدة العوام عن تشويش المبتدعة كما أن حد البذرقة حراسة أقمشة الحجيج عن نهب العرب ورددت حد الفقيه إلى حفظ القانون الذي به يكف السلطان شر بعض أهل العدوان عن بعض وهاتان رتبتان نازلتان بالإضافة إلى علم الدين وعلماء الأمة المشهورون بالفضل هم الفقهاء والمتكلمون وهم أفضل الخلق عند الله تعالى فكيف تنزل درجاتهم إلى هذه المنزلة السافلة بالإضافة إلى علم الدين فاعلم أن من عرف الحق بالرجال حار في متاهات الضلال فاعرف الحق تع", "english": "And al-Junayd — may God have mercy on him — said: My shaykh al-Sarī said to me one day: \"When you leave my presence, whom do you sit with?\" I replied: \"Al-Muḥāsibī.\" He said: \"Yes. Take from his knowledge and his refinement, but leave aside his hair-splitting speech and his refutations against the theologians (mutakallimūn).\" Then, when I turned away, I heard him say: \"May God make you a traditionist who is a Sufi, and not a Sufi who is a traditionist.\" He pointed thereby to the fact that whoever first acquires the ḥadīth and knowledge, and then becomes a Sufi, succeeds; but whoever becomes a Sufi before knowledge imperils his own soul.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 17", "section_title_en": "The Station of the Jurists and Theologians Relative to the Science of Religion — Section 17", "section_index": 17, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the faculty of perception and discernment; the act of grasping a thing's reality.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of essence and judgment of existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal knowledge (maʿrifah) from the metaphorical \"awareness\" (shiʿr); here, the \"science of the Hereafter\" (ʿilm al-ākhirah) is the true knowledge of God, not mere discursive theology.\n- Root: بدع (badaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To originate something without prior model; innovation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبداع: إنشاء صنعة بلا احتذاء واقتداء\" — creation without precedent; when applied to God, it is bringing into being without instrument, matter, time, or place.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to invent or initiate. Metaphorical: \"أبدعت حجتك\" — your argument became weak. Here, the \"innovation\" (bidʿah) that diverts from the Qur'an and Sunnah is the blameworthy innovation that necessitated the science of theology (kalām) as a defensive necessity.\n- Root: طرق (ṭaraqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To strike, to beat; a path beaten by feet.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطريق: السبيل الذي يطرق بالأرجل\" — the path trodden by feet; metaphorically, any course of action, praiseworthy or blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: striking iron with a hammer. Metaphorical: \"طرقني الخيال\" — the phantom visited me at night. Here, the \"way of the Hereafter\" (ṭarīq al-ākhirah) is the path of spiritual striving (mujāhadah), not the beaten track of disputation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to confine the true rank of the jurist and theologian to the role of guardian, not the possessor of the science of the Hereafter.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 350, 7081]], "2": [[1, 400, 1515], [2, 1515, 3149], [3, 3149, 3706], [4, 3706, 6240], [5, 6240, 6797], [6, 6797, 9508], [7, 9508, 11396], [8, 11396, 12258], [9, 12258, 12917], [10, 12917, 14691]], "3": [[1, 400, 1812], [2, 1812, 3538], [3, 3538, 5149], [4, 5149, 5719], [5, 5719, 7245], [6, 7245, 9855], [7, 9855, 10982], [8, 10982, 12593], [9, 12593, 14475], [10, 14475, 17227], [11, 17227, 19367]], "4": [[1, 400, 1010], [2, 1010, 1898], [3, 1898, 3466], [4, 3466, 5464], [5, 5464, 7892], [6, 7892, 8502], [7, 8502, 10972], [8, 10972, 12654], [9, 12654, 13375], [10, 13375, 15345]], "5": [[1, 400, 949], [2, 949, 1937], [3, 1937, 3749], [4, 3749, 5715], [5, 5715, 6799], [6, 6799, 8076], [7, 8076, 8955], [8, 8955, 10039], [9, 10039, 11247], [10, 11247, 12318], [11, 12318, 14219], [12, 14219, 15867]], "6": [[1, 400, 1478], [2, 1478, 2556], [3, 2556, 4789], [4, 4789, 6329], [5, 6329, 8313], [6, 8313, 9437], [7, 9437, 10654], [8, 10654, 12418], [9, 12418, 13096], [10, 13096, 15475]], "7": [[1, 400, 2639], [2, 2639, 4987], [3, 4987, 7429], [4, 7429, 10153], [